{"parentUuid":"ffe665f0-86f3-48a9-9c18-ce43cde69cff","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d3b98ac2-d94a-4ded-8d2a-6ab7c83cd263","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MsBKNYeiCfkQ1cdU4vac5V","type":"tool_result","content":"You are an independent reviewer in Flint's ingest review gate. Another model wrote these entries; your job is to FALSIFY them, not confirm. Read-only on entries and sources. Write only your report file.\n\nStandards: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (read it first). Every rule there is a check.\n\nFor EVERY entry in scope:\n1. Every number, definition, table value and quote appears at the cited PDF page (and printed page) and means the same thing. Open the pages yourself; do not trust the entry.\n2. Attribution: plain text is the book author's own statement. Anything else must be labelled \"Eric's baseline:\", \"Eric's review:\" or \"Inference:\". Flag any unlabelled claim the author did not make, any \"why it works\" mechanism the author did not give, and any invented example.\n3. Confidence: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics, so challenge every `proven`. Soviet-era or single-lab findings and prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for anecdotes. `contested` needs a stated reason.\n4. \"When NOT to apply\" is present and substantive.\n5. Duplicates: flag any entry that restates an existing vault entry (grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library`) without a new angle.\n6. Missing: up to 5 important ideas in the covered pages that no entry captures.\n\nOutput (Markdown): a table with one row per entry: file | verdict (GO / FIX / NO-GO) | problem, quoting the entry and the source with its page | exact correction. For GO rows, list what you checked (\"checked: 2.5x at PDF 23 / p.12\"). Then the missing list. Then an overall verdict: PROMOTE, GO AFTER FIXES (list them), or NO-GO.\n# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n\nStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n\n## Voices: label every one\n\n- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n\n## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n\n- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d25c7404-bfdd-43a1-8a9c-adf40bf9d5fa","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:05.194Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"You are an independent reviewer in Flint's ingest review gate. Another model wrote these entries; your job is to FALSIFY them, not confirm. Read-only on entries and sources. Write only your report file.\n\nStandards: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (read it first). Every rule there is a check.\n\nFor EVERY entry in scope:\n1. Every number, definition, table value and quote appears at the cited PDF page (and printed page) and means the same thing. Open the pages yourself; do not trust the entry.\n2. Attribution: plain text is the book author's own statement. Anything else must be labelled \"Eric's baseline:\", \"Eric's review:\" or \"Inference:\". Flag any unlabelled claim the author did not make, any \"why it works\" mechanism the author did not give, and any invented example.\n3. Confidence: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics, so challenge every `proven`. Soviet-era or single-lab findings and prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for anecdotes. `contested` needs a stated reason.\n4. \"When NOT to apply\" is present and substantive.\n5. Duplicates: flag any entry that restates an existing vault entry (grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library`) without a new angle.\n6. Missing: up to 5 important ideas in the covered pages that no entry captures.\n\nOutput (Markdown): a table with one row per entry: file | verdict (GO / FIX / NO-GO) | problem, quoting the entry and the source with its page | exact correction. For GO rows, list what you checked (\"checked: 2.5x at PDF 23 / p.12\"). Then the missing list. Then an overall verdict: PROMOTE, GO AFTER FIXES (list them), or NO-GO.\n# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n\nStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n\n## Voices: label every one\n\n- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n\n## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n\n- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ffe665f0-86f3-48a9-9c18-ce43cde69cff","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d25c7404-bfdd-43a1-8a9c-adf40bf9d5fa","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffp8a1o4iM2RHTnRY42K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VXjbujqH3yB71Lchgn6w5m","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5575,"cache_read_input_tokens":50323,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5575,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VXjbujqH3yB71Lchgn6w5m":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffp8ZnQyaPttsWLVwD82","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"806b061f-5cec-4947-ba23-3ed18b9ba6de","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:05.337Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"806b061f-5cec-4947-ba23-3ed18b9ba6de","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d3b98ac2-d94a-4ded-8d2a-6ab7c83cd263","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VXjbujqH3yB71Lchgn6w5m","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6, batch 6b (sections 6.4 to 6.8.2)\n2\t\n3\tReviewer: Claude Opus, independent falsification pass, 2026-10-03.\n4\tScope: the 16 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`.\n5\tSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 347-362 (printed 336-351), plus PDF 305 and 381.\n6\t\n7\tMethod: I extracted the text layer for PDF 305, 347-362 and 381. I rendered and opened at 200 dpi PDF 348, 349, 351, 353, 354, 357, 358, 359, 360, 361 and 362, and the middle of 381. I also cropped Fig 6.22 and both panels of Fig 6.23 at 400 dpi and read values with pixel measurements (Fig 6.23 A: 11.12 px per 1%; B: 11.13 px per 1%; 64.3 to 64.6 px per week). I checked all 19 cited `method-baseline.md` lines (27, 30, 32, 59, 64, 75, 101, 105, 106, 115, 116, 117, 123, 151, 153, 171, 183, 185, 204). Every quote and tag matches. All 62 wikilink targets resolve. Each file has exactly six em dashes, all in the section headers, and no wikilink is wrapped across lines. Renders are in `staging/_renders/r6b_*.png`.\n8\t\n9\t**PDF 359:** The writer read this page from the text layer only. I opened the page image, and the text layer matches it word for word for every quote used. (The adaptation definition, \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process ...\", is italic in the original.) The fixer can change \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" in the four entries that cite PDF 359.\n10\t\n11\t**PDF 381:** I checked the image. The text says \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" for unidirectional training and \"up to 2 months\" for concentrated loading.\n12\t\n13\t## Entries\n14\t\n15\t| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n16\t|---|---|---|---|\n17\t| framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions | FIX | (1) The entry says \"Athlete's fitness state (shaded box A) ... Athlete's external interactions (shaded box B)\". The Fig 6.12 image (PDF 348 / p.337) shows box A as the whole shaded band {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}. Box B is the larger shaded region {Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions}, with fitness state in both. The text's subsystem list is right; only the figure walk-through mislabels the boxes. (2) Why it works says \"fitness state is the only route from load to result, so both ... links must be measured\" and presents this as the authors' reason. PDF 347-348 gives the one-way chain and the two control levels, but neither \"only route\" nor \"must be measured\" is stated. Checked and correct: all the 6.4 quotes (PDF 347-348), the Fig 6.12 box names and the arrow from Sports Competition and Laboratory Tests into Assessment of Effect, the \"lower the measures for the first one (A)\" quote, and baseline lines 64 and 32. | (1) Replace the parentheticals with: \"the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state\". (2) Change the Why-it-works lead to: \"The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF 348). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring.\" |\n18\t| heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation | GO | Checked: the \"individual fragments of research\" opening, the \"All too often ... single factor\", \"very rarely\" and specialist quotes (PDF 349 / p.338), \"cannot be condemned ... 'their' types of sport\", the coach-scientist passage and the VO2max fall in the competition period (PDF 350 / p.339), and every Fig 6.13 box against the image (PDF 349). The linked VO2max claim does carry the competition-period fall (PDF 259-260). Baseline lines 27 and 30 (Explicit) match. The When NOT section is substantive. `untested` is right. | None. |\n19\t| heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity | FIX | (1) Fig 6.15 description: the entry says \"a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.\" In the image (PDF 351 / p.340), B is not a separate spike. It is a second hump on the right-hand descending limb of the broad inverted-U, which matches the text's \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B)\". Only A and C are separate narrow spikes. (2) Borrowed models: the entry correctly names Yerkes and Dodson as the source of the curve. The introvert/extrovert arousal claim in point 2 is also a borrowed idea, but the book names no source for it, and the entry lists it under \"The authors' points\" with nothing to say it is not their own finding. Checked and correct: every other quote on PDF 351 and the PDF 352 carry-over (\"overstress\", \"supercompensation\"), the \"(Siff, 1990)\" note on PDF 350, baseline line 153, the Example's golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting, and `untested`. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.15 sentence with: \"one broad inverted-U over 'low / optimal / high' arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve ('second wind'); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.\" (2) In point 2, add: \"The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim ('appears'), not as the authors' finding.\" Optional provenance notes (Eysenck for the arousal and personality idea, the 1908 Yerkes-Dodson task) need a checked citation before they are added. |\n20\t| heuristic--expectancy-motivation-break-distant-goals-into-attainable-steps | GO | Checked against the PDF 353 / p.342 image: the \"Vroom's Expectancy Theory of Motivation\" naming, \"(Tellier, 1978)\", the V and B definitions, F = V x B, the -5 to +5 and 0 to 1 scales, the sum over outcomes, \"Detailed examples ... outside the scope of this book\", and the leader quote. The model is named as Vroom's, and the scales are reported as given, not as the authors' finding. The entry flags the single-product versus sum wording correctly. Baseline line 171 (Inferred) matches. Example is labelled Inference. `untested` is right. | None. Optional: say that the two-factor form and the scales are as reported through Tellier, so later readers do not cite them as Vroom's original formulation. |\n21\t| framework--situational-leadership-coach-matches-style-to-athlete-maturity | GO | Checked against the Fig 6.19 image (PDF 354 / p.343). Relationship behaviour is on the vertical axis and task behaviour on the horizontal. The quadrants are S3 Participating (top left), S2 Selling (top right), S4 Delegating (bottom left) and S1 Telling (bottom right). There is a bell curve, and the maturity strip runs M4 to M1 from left to right, so M1 pairs with S1. The quotes (\"ought to be implemented according to each situation\", the maturity list, \"low, moderate or high (M1, M2, M3 or M4)\", \"hence the shape of the curve\") all match. The model is credited to Hersey and Blanchard, and the three-words-for-four-codes gap is flagged. Baseline line 204 matches. `untested` is right. | None. Minor: the Why-it-works clause \"a follower low in experience ... needs direction\" is the model's logic read off the figure; prefixing \"Inference:\" would be cleaner. |\n22\t| framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming | FIX | When NOT says \"the authors' own call to 'further subdivide' applies\" to positions within the complex group. On PDF 357 / p.346 (image) the call (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") comes after the acyclic and cyclic bullets and before \"Finally, one should identify a third group\". It is made for the two broad groups only, and the complex group is never subdivided. Checked and correct: all the 6.7 quotes (PDF 356-357), including \"the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions\" (in the third-group paragraph); Farfel (1969); the 100 m example; baseline lines 115, 116 and 117, with quotes and tags matching. The source-against-baseline contrast on football is fairly stated. | Change the sentence to: \"Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough ... that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision only within the acyclic and cyclic groups (PDF 357); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference.\" |\n23\t| principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"The authors' reason is that the effects they care about ... unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them.\" PDF 358 / p.347 gives \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" as the basis and calls for \"An extensive study of ... long-term adaptation\". The weeks-long-effects mechanism is the writer's. (2) When NOT contains process notes: \"Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3 ...) may carry an overlapping entry ...\". The brief forbids review or process history in entries. (3) Missing tension: the 6.2 entry `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap` quotes the book calling the training session \"the fundamental unit\" (PDF 327 / p.316), while 6.8.1 says the fundamental unit \"should be the mesocycle\". Checked and correct: the three bulleted recommendations, the Folbrot and Weigert citations and the verdict quote (PDF 358 image), and baseline lines 59 and 106. | (1) Rewrite as: \"The authors' stated basis is 'modern sporting requirements' and 'common sense' (PDF 358); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\" (2) Delete the batch 6a sentence. Replace it with: \"The book calls the training session 'the fundamental unit' of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF 327 / p.316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1; the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording.\" Link [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]. |\n24\t| claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation | FIX | **Figure misread under a false verification claim.** The entry says it checked Fig 6.22 \"against the page image and a high-resolution crop\" and that \"in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak\". It treats this as figure-against-text. My 400 dpi crop of PDF 358 / p.347 shows graph A's rise shrinking each year: about 220 px in Year 1, 145 px in Year 2 and 65 px in Year 3 (slopes of roughly 0.39, 0.25 and 0.11). That is a decelerating curve, consistent with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF 359). No peak is reached within the three years drawn, which the text does not claim either. The contradiction does not exist. Checked and correct: all four conclusions word for word (PDF 358 image), the B and C assignment to bi-cyclic and mono-cyclic, the A and D descriptions, the endurance caveat (PDF 359, now image-checked), and baseline lines 64 and 185. `supported` is right. | Replace \"a thick, nearly straight rising line A\" with \"a thick rising line A whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\". Delete the \"Figure against text\" sentences in When NOT. Replace them with: \"Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's parabola (PDF 359); no peak is drawn within the three years.\" Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\". |\n25\t| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation | FIX | (1) The same Fig 6.22 misread: When NOT says \"Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". As shown above, A decelerates and matches the parabola. (2) Why it works says endurance adaptation \"has to be taken much further\". On PDF 359 that phrase is about research, not adaptation: \"The research done by Folbrot (1941), Selye ... and others has to be taken much further in its application to ... especially endurance sports.\" (3) \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\": now checked against the image, and every quote matches. Checked and correct: the compensatory and pre-adaptation quotes, Kuznyetsov 1979, the Bauer and Bernstein definition, the heterochronicity bullets, \"parabola\", \"genetically predetermined\" and CAR (all PDF 359 image). The genetic-limit inference is labelled. | (1) Delete \"and Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". Optionally add: \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola.\" (2) Change to: \"... and that research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) 'has to be taken much further' for endurance sports; until then they assume 'the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...'\". (3) Change the locator to \"checked against the page image\" and delete the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence. |\n26\t| principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window | GO | Checked: the CAR quotes on PDF 359 (image) and PDF 360 (image), Letunov 1952, Prokor 1959 (the book's spelling), the re-adaptation definition at the top of PDF 360, Matveyev 1964, and Beckman 1961 and Yakovlev 1977. The Chapter 1 two-definitions point is confirmed in the vault `claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation`. Baseline line 75 matches (Explicit Q2, Q2a). `supported` is right. When NOT is substantive. | None required. Minor: the PDF 359 locator can now read \"checked against the page image\". The Bompa entry describes a *standard* load leading to a plateau, not a below-threshold one, so \"(below threshold, plateau ...)\" should read \"(an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation)\". |\n27\t| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation | FIX | **Fig 6.23 values: all verified.** Panel A ends at about 144, 128, 114, 108 and 105%. A hump peaks at about 113% near week 8.8, then falls to the lowest end value; by end position and label order (maximum strength, absolute strength, speed) that is speed, and the entry's \"by that reading\" caveat is fair. Panel B lows run from about 83% to 96% at weeks 4-5. Curves cross back above 100% at about weeks 7-9 and end at about 157, 142-143, 129 (peak of about 130-131 near week 16.5), 120 and 114%. Dashed lines sit at weeks 6 and 12. Which curve dips deepest cannot be settled because the curves cross at weeks 7-9, as the entry says. **Problem 1:** When NOT has \"Figure against text: the figure's depressed phase (below 100% until about weeks 7-9) runs longer than the 3-4 week concentrated block\". This compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities. The text on the same page says the concentrated volume sits \"in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360 image), and a dip through weeks 7-9 of 18 is that first half. Indicators are expected to stay low until volume drops. **Problem 2:** The Issurin (2016) critique is stated accurately: insufficient evidence for broad use in combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, with fitness gains but no reported effect on team practice or competition (p.6), and better suited to narrow target-quality sports (p.11), all as in the vault brief. But the entry does not say whose model is being criticised; per the brief, it is Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R. **Problem 3:** \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\". PDF 360 says the method \"was devised for highly qualified athletes\"; it does not forbid it for medium-level athletes. And 6.9.5 (`claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`, PDF 371) says \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\". Checked and correct: all the PDF 360-361 quotes, Komarova 1984 (\"he errs\"), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). `contested` stands, for the Issurin reason. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.23 \"figure against text\" sentence with: \"Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's 'concentrated in the first half of the training phase' (PDF 360). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page. Its x-axis ends at 18 weeks; the text's period is 18-22 weeks.\" (2) Change \"Issurin (2016) judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence ...\" to \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model ...\". (3) Replace the last sentence with: \"The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 they say both forms work at medium qualification (PDF 371), so it is not required there.\" Add the reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]. |\n28\t| heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery | FIX | **`contested` is justified, but the conflict mixes up different quantities.** Sorted by what each number measures: (a) **Concentrated block or period length:** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360 / p.349, image); \"4-8 week concentrated blocks\" (PDF 305 / p.294); \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF 381 / p.370, image). These are the same quantity, so the conflict is real. (b) **Unidirectional training stage length:** \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks\" (PDF 305) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF 381). This is a stage of one emphasis, not a concentrated block. (c) **Delayed-effect duration:** 4-12 weeks (PDF 375, recorded in the vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next`). (d) **Latency to gains:** 1-3 weeks (PDF 305). (e) **Stage to exhaust the CAR:** 18-22 weeks (PDF 360). (f) **Upper limit for one primary emphasis:** months, by quality (staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, PDF 370-373). The entry files the 4-12 week figure under \"Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration)\", next to the 4-8 weeks, as if both were block lengths. Checked and correct: every numbered quote (PDF 360 image), Zhikharevich 1976 and Sirenko 1980, the arithmetic (17-21 and 11-15 weeks), and baseline line 183. The Issurin (six weeks) and Bondarchuk (four-week blocks) Related notes match the vault files. | Rewrite the When NOT conflict paragraph as: \"**Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** 3-4 weeks here (PDF 360 / p.349); '4-8 week concentrated blocks' in 5.3.3 (PDF 305 / p.294); 'a limited period (of up to 2 months)' in 6.12.2 (PDF 381 / p.370). **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage* (PDF 305, 381); 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts (PDF 375, see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]); 1-3 weeks is the latency to gains (PDF 305); 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF 360).\" Keep the \"three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks\" reconciliation, labelled Inference, as a link between block length (a) and stage length (b). Add as Inference: \"5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (23-25% of the annual volume, PDF 305, 381), which makes the month the working unit; 'up to 2 months' reads as a ceiling.\" Add the link [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. |\n29\t| heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance | GO | Checked: the full restorative-stage quote against the PDF 361 / p.350 image, the \"after the volume of loading is decreased\" quote (PDF 360), the 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks (PDF 305), and the 7-10 day pause (PDF 360). The Bompa & Haff quote matches the vault `allow-restoration` entry word for word. Baseline line 151 (Inferred) matches. The GPP mechanism is correctly marked as not given. The Example is labelled Inference. `supported` is right. | None. Minor: \"as quoted in the 5.3.3 entry above\" should read \"as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]\", since \"above\" means nothing in a standalone note. |\n30\t| claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect | FIX | (1) Process note in the entry body: \"The Table 6.1 and 6.2.2 material it points to is in batch 6a's pages (PDF pp. 334-336), not checked here.\" The brief forbids review or process history. (I confirmed that 6.2.2 starts on PDF 334 and Table 6.1 is referenced on PDF 335.) (2) Why it works opens \"Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading\". That is a correct observation, but it is the writer's, presented without a label. Checked and correct: the full quote, including the italic \"the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity\" (PDF 361 image), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). The tension with the PDF 360 \"more extensive ... the longer the new functional level is maintained\" claim is accurate and labelled. `untested` is acceptable: one group, reported second-hand, with Bondarchuk's own \"strictly\" caveat. | (1) Replace the batch sentence with: \"The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-336 / printed pp. 323-325.\" (2) Prefix \"Inference:\" to the first sentence of Why it works. |\n31\t| principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities.\" PDF 362 / p.351 (image) points only to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3, and supports the cyclic point with Zhurbinoi (1978). There is no Chapter 4 pointer on the page. (2) \"(Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" is attached to \"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\". In the source, the citation closes the *next* sentence (\"Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength ... (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\"). Checked and correct: the 6.8.2 quotes (PDF 361-362 images), the \"classification scheme of 6.5\" note (a correct catch: the scheme is in 6.7), Sargent 1926, Henry 1951, Margaria 1963, and the triple jump example. Baseline line 123 (Inferred) and line 116 (Goals 1 and 2 are alactic and aerobic) match. | (1) Rewrite as: \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF 362). Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point.\" (2) Quote both sentences with the citation where the source puts it, or write: \"strength preparedness ... plays an important role\", followed by a study note (Zhurbinoi, 1978). |\n32\t| claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov | FIX | The title says \"Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top\". The source (PDF 362, image) says \"distance athletes\", not runners. The entry body rightly calls the sentence \"Unclear as printed\", but the title settles that ambiguity as fact (\"Add\"). Checked and correct: the full Vankov quote, the class-level expansions, the swimmer comparison, the convergence link (labelled Inference), and baseline line 101 (Explicit Q1). `supported` is right. | Change the title to: \"Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\". Keep the body's unclear-as-printed note as it is. |\n33\t\n34\tCounts: **GO 5, FIX 11, NO-GO 0.**\n35\t\n36\t## Special-attention findings\n37\t\n38\t- **Borrowed management theory.** All three entries name whose model it is: Yerkes and Dodson for the arousal curve, Vroom through Tellier (1978) for expectancy, and Hersey and Blanchard for situational leadership. None presents the general model as the authors' finding. The `untested` ratings are right, since the book gives no evidence for any of them. The one gap is the unsourced introvert/extrovert claim (arousal entry, FIX 2).\n39\t- **Distributed versus concentrated `contested`.** The Issurin critique is stated accurately against the vault brief (p.6 and p.11) and is aimed at the concentrated-unidirectional model. The entry should say that model is Verkhoshansky's. The figure-against-text claim compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities (FIX).\n40\t- **Block-durations `contested`.** The rating holds: 3-4 weeks (PDF 360), 4-8 weeks (PDF 305) and \"up to 2 months\" (PDF 381) all measure concentrated block or period length. The 4-12 week figures measure something else (stage length at PDF 305 and 381, delayed-effect duration at PDF 375), and so does the 18-22 week figure (the stage needed to exhaust the CAR). The entry mixes these together (FIX).\n41\t- **Fig 6.22.** Graph A decelerates; it is not straight. Two entries assert a contradiction with the text that does not exist, and claim a high-resolution crop check. This is the most serious error in the batch.\n42\t- **Fig 6.23.** Every value the entries use is correct to within about 1 percentage point. The writer's panel A uncertainty is handled honestly. The panel B uncertainty about which curve dips deepest cannot be settled from the image.\n43\t- **Baseline lines 116-117 (football energy systems).** Both are quoted accurately with the correct tags in `three-sport-groups`. Line 116 is quoted accurately in `functional-specialisation`. The source-against-baseline contrast on glycolytic share is presented fairly.\n44\t\n45\t## Missing (up to 5)\n46\t\n47\t1. **Decision-making and problem-solving (PDF 355-356 / p.344-345):** rate each problem high, medium or low on seriousness, urgency and growth; the six problem-solving steps; Maier's (1963) quality-versus-acceptance split and Roskin's four decision styles. This is the most directly usable part of 6.6 for running a department.\n48\t2. **Management versus leadership (PDF 350, 353):** Tellier's management roles (responsibility, authority, accountability) set against leadership as influence (expertise, personality, charisma), and Oncken's four types of authority (position, personality, competence, integrity).\n49\t3. **Blake-Mouton managerial grid and the Tannenbaum-Schmidt continuum with Likert's styles (PDF 353-355, Figs 6.17, 6.18 and 6.20):** the people-versus-production grid coordinates (9,1), (1,9), (1,1), (9,9) and (5,5). This could be one framework entry beside situational leadership.\n50\t4. **Needs theories (PDF 352):** Maslow, Alderfer's ERG and McClelland's achievement, power and affiliation, with the authors' warning that Maslow's hierarchy \"has to be applied with circumspection\", and the transcendence and athletes note (Murphy & White, 1978).\n51\t5. **The endurance monitoring limit (PDF 359):** \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity ... but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation.\" This is a monitoring point in its own right, and the compensatory entry mentions it only in passing.\n52\t\n53\t## Duplicates and reciprocal links\n54\t\n55\tThere are no duplicates. A grep of the vault and staging for Yerkes, Vroom, Hersey, Farfel, \"fundamental unit\", CAR, distributed loading, Bondarchuk 50% and Vankov found no entry that restates any of these 16. Suggested reciprocal links:\n56\t\n57\t- `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading...` and staging 6.9.5 `claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`.\n58\t- `heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks...` and staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, plus vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` (for the PDF 375 delayed-effect duration).\n59\t- `principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle...` and staging 6.2 `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap`.\n60\t- `principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window` and staging 6.9.4 `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`.\n61\t\n62\t## Overall verdict\n63\t\n64\t**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n65\t\n66\t1. Fig 6.22 misread in `four-observations` and `compensatory-adaptation`: graph A decelerates, so remove the figure-against-text claim.\n67\t2. `distributed-vs-concentrated`: replace the Fig 6.23 dip-versus-block comparison; name Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model in the Issurin line; correct \"not for medium-level\".\n68\t3. `concentrated-blocks`: sort the conflict by quantity (block 3-4 / 4-8 weeks / up to 2 months versus stage 4-12 weeks, delayed effect 4-12 weeks, CAR stage 18-22 weeks).\n69\t4. Remove the batch-6a process notes from `mesocycle` and `bondarchuk`.\n70\t5. Label the writer's reasoning as Inference: `mesocycle` Why it works, `functional-specialisation` Chapter 4 pointer, `bondarchuk` Why it works opener, `logical-scheme` \"only route\".\n71\t6. Small source mismatches: `three-sport-groups` \"further subdivide\", `compensatory` \"taken much further\", `vankov` title, `arousal` curve B and the unsourced personality claim, `logical-scheme` shaded boxes, `functional-specialisation` Zhurbinoi placement.\n72\t7. Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" (I have verified the page).\n73\t"}]},"uuid":"24154901-f597-4f41-b001-deff9a1a3f09","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:05.709Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md","content":"# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6, batch 6b (sections 6.4 to 6.8.2)\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus, independent falsification pass, 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 16 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 347-362 (printed 336-351), plus PDF 305 and 381.\n\nMethod: I extracted the text layer for PDF 305, 347-362 and 381. I rendered and opened at 200 dpi PDF 348, 349, 351, 353, 354, 357, 358, 359, 360, 361 and 362, and the middle of 381. I also cropped Fig 6.22 and both panels of Fig 6.23 at 400 dpi and read values with pixel measurements (Fig 6.23 A: 11.12 px per 1%; B: 11.13 px per 1%; 64.3 to 64.6 px per week). I checked all 19 cited `method-baseline.md` lines (27, 30, 32, 59, 64, 75, 101, 105, 106, 115, 116, 117, 123, 151, 153, 171, 183, 185, 204). Every quote and tag matches. All 62 wikilink targets resolve. Each file has exactly six em dashes, all in the section headers, and no wikilink is wrapped across lines. Renders are in `staging/_renders/r6b_*.png`.\n\n**PDF 359:** The writer read this page from the text layer only. I opened the page image, and the text layer matches it word for word for every quote used. (The adaptation definition, \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process ...\", is italic in the original.) The fixer can change \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" in the four entries that cite PDF 359.\n\n**PDF 381:** I checked the image. The text says \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" for unidirectional training and \"up to 2 months\" for concentrated loading.\n\n## Entries\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions | FIX | (1) The entry says \"Athlete's fitness state (shaded box A) ... Athlete's external interactions (shaded box B)\". The Fig 6.12 image (PDF 348 / p.337) shows box A as the whole shaded band {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}. Box B is the larger shaded region {Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions}, with fitness state in both. The text's subsystem list is right; only the figure walk-through mislabels the boxes. (2) Why it works says \"fitness state is the only route from load to result, so both ... links must be measured\" and presents this as the authors' reason. PDF 347-348 gives the one-way chain and the two control levels, but neither \"only route\" nor \"must be measured\" is stated. Checked and correct: all the 6.4 quotes (PDF 347-348), the Fig 6.12 box names and the arrow from Sports Competition and Laboratory Tests into Assessment of Effect, the \"lower the measures for the first one (A)\" quote, and baseline lines 64 and 32. | (1) Replace the parentheticals with: \"the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state\". (2) Change the Why-it-works lead to: \"The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF 348). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring.\" |\n| heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation | GO | Checked: the \"individual fragments of research\" opening, the \"All too often ... single factor\", \"very rarely\" and specialist quotes (PDF 349 / p.338), \"cannot be condemned ... 'their' types of sport\", the coach-scientist passage and the VO2max fall in the competition period (PDF 350 / p.339), and every Fig 6.13 box against the image (PDF 349). The linked VO2max claim does carry the competition-period fall (PDF 259-260). Baseline lines 27 and 30 (Explicit) match. The When NOT section is substantive. `untested` is right. | None. |\n| heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity | FIX | (1) Fig 6.15 description: the entry says \"a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.\" In the image (PDF 351 / p.340), B is not a separate spike. It is a second hump on the right-hand descending limb of the broad inverted-U, which matches the text's \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B)\". Only A and C are separate narrow spikes. (2) Borrowed models: the entry correctly names Yerkes and Dodson as the source of the curve. The introvert/extrovert arousal claim in point 2 is also a borrowed idea, but the book names no source for it, and the entry lists it under \"The authors' points\" with nothing to say it is not their own finding. Checked and correct: every other quote on PDF 351 and the PDF 352 carry-over (\"overstress\", \"supercompensation\"), the \"(Siff, 1990)\" note on PDF 350, baseline line 153, the Example's golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting, and `untested`. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.15 sentence with: \"one broad inverted-U over 'low / optimal / high' arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve ('second wind'); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.\" (2) In point 2, add: \"The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim ('appears'), not as the authors' finding.\" Optional provenance notes (Eysenck for the arousal and personality idea, the 1908 Yerkes-Dodson task) need a checked citation before they are added. |\n| heuristic--expectancy-motivation-break-distant-goals-into-attainable-steps | GO | Checked against the PDF 353 / p.342 image: the \"Vroom's Expectancy Theory of Motivation\" naming, \"(Tellier, 1978)\", the V and B definitions, F = V x B, the -5 to +5 and 0 to 1 scales, the sum over outcomes, \"Detailed examples ... outside the scope of this book\", and the leader quote. The model is named as Vroom's, and the scales are reported as given, not as the authors' finding. The entry flags the single-product versus sum wording correctly. Baseline line 171 (Inferred) matches. Example is labelled Inference. `untested` is right. | None. Optional: say that the two-factor form and the scales are as reported through Tellier, so later readers do not cite them as Vroom's original formulation. |\n| framework--situational-leadership-coach-matches-style-to-athlete-maturity | GO | Checked against the Fig 6.19 image (PDF 354 / p.343). Relationship behaviour is on the vertical axis and task behaviour on the horizontal. The quadrants are S3 Participating (top left), S2 Selling (top right), S4 Delegating (bottom left) and S1 Telling (bottom right). There is a bell curve, and the maturity strip runs M4 to M1 from left to right, so M1 pairs with S1. The quotes (\"ought to be implemented according to each situation\", the maturity list, \"low, moderate or high (M1, M2, M3 or M4)\", \"hence the shape of the curve\") all match. The model is credited to Hersey and Blanchard, and the three-words-for-four-codes gap is flagged. Baseline line 204 matches. `untested` is right. | None. Minor: the Why-it-works clause \"a follower low in experience ... needs direction\" is the model's logic read off the figure; prefixing \"Inference:\" would be cleaner. |\n| framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming | FIX | When NOT says \"the authors' own call to 'further subdivide' applies\" to positions within the complex group. On PDF 357 / p.346 (image) the call (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") comes after the acyclic and cyclic bullets and before \"Finally, one should identify a third group\". It is made for the two broad groups only, and the complex group is never subdivided. Checked and correct: all the 6.7 quotes (PDF 356-357), including \"the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions\" (in the third-group paragraph); Farfel (1969); the 100 m example; baseline lines 115, 116 and 117, with quotes and tags matching. The source-against-baseline contrast on football is fairly stated. | Change the sentence to: \"Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough ... that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision only within the acyclic and cyclic groups (PDF 357); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference.\" |\n| principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"The authors' reason is that the effects they care about ... unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them.\" PDF 358 / p.347 gives \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" as the basis and calls for \"An extensive study of ... long-term adaptation\". The weeks-long-effects mechanism is the writer's. (2) When NOT contains process notes: \"Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3 ...) may carry an overlapping entry ...\". The brief forbids review or process history in entries. (3) Missing tension: the 6.2 entry `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap` quotes the book calling the training session \"the fundamental unit\" (PDF 327 / p.316), while 6.8.1 says the fundamental unit \"should be the mesocycle\". Checked and correct: the three bulleted recommendations, the Folbrot and Weigert citations and the verdict quote (PDF 358 image), and baseline lines 59 and 106. | (1) Rewrite as: \"The authors' stated basis is 'modern sporting requirements' and 'common sense' (PDF 358); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\" (2) Delete the batch 6a sentence. Replace it with: \"The book calls the training session 'the fundamental unit' of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF 327 / p.316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1; the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording.\" Link [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]. |\n| claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation | FIX | **Figure misread under a false verification claim.** The entry says it checked Fig 6.22 \"against the page image and a high-resolution crop\" and that \"in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak\". It treats this as figure-against-text. My 400 dpi crop of PDF 358 / p.347 shows graph A's rise shrinking each year: about 220 px in Year 1, 145 px in Year 2 and 65 px in Year 3 (slopes of roughly 0.39, 0.25 and 0.11). That is a decelerating curve, consistent with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF 359). No peak is reached within the three years drawn, which the text does not claim either. The contradiction does not exist. Checked and correct: all four conclusions word for word (PDF 358 image), the B and C assignment to bi-cyclic and mono-cyclic, the A and D descriptions, the endurance caveat (PDF 359, now image-checked), and baseline lines 64 and 185. `supported` is right. | Replace \"a thick, nearly straight rising line A\" with \"a thick rising line A whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\". Delete the \"Figure against text\" sentences in When NOT. Replace them with: \"Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's parabola (PDF 359); no peak is drawn within the three years.\" Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\". |\n| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation | FIX | (1) The same Fig 6.22 misread: When NOT says \"Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". As shown above, A decelerates and matches the parabola. (2) Why it works says endurance adaptation \"has to be taken much further\". On PDF 359 that phrase is about research, not adaptation: \"The research done by Folbrot (1941), Selye ... and others has to be taken much further in its application to ... especially endurance sports.\" (3) \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\": now checked against the image, and every quote matches. Checked and correct: the compensatory and pre-adaptation quotes, Kuznyetsov 1979, the Bauer and Bernstein definition, the heterochronicity bullets, \"parabola\", \"genetically predetermined\" and CAR (all PDF 359 image). The genetic-limit inference is labelled. | (1) Delete \"and Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". Optionally add: \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola.\" (2) Change to: \"... and that research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) 'has to be taken much further' for endurance sports; until then they assume 'the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...'\". (3) Change the locator to \"checked against the page image\" and delete the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence. |\n| principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window | GO | Checked: the CAR quotes on PDF 359 (image) and PDF 360 (image), Letunov 1952, Prokor 1959 (the book's spelling), the re-adaptation definition at the top of PDF 360, Matveyev 1964, and Beckman 1961 and Yakovlev 1977. The Chapter 1 two-definitions point is confirmed in the vault `claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation`. Baseline line 75 matches (Explicit Q2, Q2a). `supported` is right. When NOT is substantive. | None required. Minor: the PDF 359 locator can now read \"checked against the page image\". The Bompa entry describes a *standard* load leading to a plateau, not a below-threshold one, so \"(below threshold, plateau ...)\" should read \"(an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation)\". |\n| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation | FIX | **Fig 6.23 values: all verified.** Panel A ends at about 144, 128, 114, 108 and 105%. A hump peaks at about 113% near week 8.8, then falls to the lowest end value; by end position and label order (maximum strength, absolute strength, speed) that is speed, and the entry's \"by that reading\" caveat is fair. Panel B lows run from about 83% to 96% at weeks 4-5. Curves cross back above 100% at about weeks 7-9 and end at about 157, 142-143, 129 (peak of about 130-131 near week 16.5), 120 and 114%. Dashed lines sit at weeks 6 and 12. Which curve dips deepest cannot be settled because the curves cross at weeks 7-9, as the entry says. **Problem 1:** When NOT has \"Figure against text: the figure's depressed phase (below 100% until about weeks 7-9) runs longer than the 3-4 week concentrated block\". This compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities. The text on the same page says the concentrated volume sits \"in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360 image), and a dip through weeks 7-9 of 18 is that first half. Indicators are expected to stay low until volume drops. **Problem 2:** The Issurin (2016) critique is stated accurately: insufficient evidence for broad use in combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, with fitness gains but no reported effect on team practice or competition (p.6), and better suited to narrow target-quality sports (p.11), all as in the vault brief. But the entry does not say whose model is being criticised; per the brief, it is Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R. **Problem 3:** \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\". PDF 360 says the method \"was devised for highly qualified athletes\"; it does not forbid it for medium-level athletes. And 6.9.5 (`claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`, PDF 371) says \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\". Checked and correct: all the PDF 360-361 quotes, Komarova 1984 (\"he errs\"), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). `contested` stands, for the Issurin reason. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.23 \"figure against text\" sentence with: \"Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's 'concentrated in the first half of the training phase' (PDF 360). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page. Its x-axis ends at 18 weeks; the text's period is 18-22 weeks.\" (2) Change \"Issurin (2016) judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence ...\" to \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model ...\". (3) Replace the last sentence with: \"The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 they say both forms work at medium qualification (PDF 371), so it is not required there.\" Add the reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]. |\n| heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery | FIX | **`contested` is justified, but the conflict mixes up different quantities.** Sorted by what each number measures: (a) **Concentrated block or period length:** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360 / p.349, image); \"4-8 week concentrated blocks\" (PDF 305 / p.294); \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF 381 / p.370, image). These are the same quantity, so the conflict is real. (b) **Unidirectional training stage length:** \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks\" (PDF 305) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF 381). This is a stage of one emphasis, not a concentrated block. (c) **Delayed-effect duration:** 4-12 weeks (PDF 375, recorded in the vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next`). (d) **Latency to gains:** 1-3 weeks (PDF 305). (e) **Stage to exhaust the CAR:** 18-22 weeks (PDF 360). (f) **Upper limit for one primary emphasis:** months, by quality (staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, PDF 370-373). The entry files the 4-12 week figure under \"Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration)\", next to the 4-8 weeks, as if both were block lengths. Checked and correct: every numbered quote (PDF 360 image), Zhikharevich 1976 and Sirenko 1980, the arithmetic (17-21 and 11-15 weeks), and baseline line 183. The Issurin (six weeks) and Bondarchuk (four-week blocks) Related notes match the vault files. | Rewrite the When NOT conflict paragraph as: \"**Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** 3-4 weeks here (PDF 360 / p.349); '4-8 week concentrated blocks' in 5.3.3 (PDF 305 / p.294); 'a limited period (of up to 2 months)' in 6.12.2 (PDF 381 / p.370). **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage* (PDF 305, 381); 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts (PDF 375, see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]); 1-3 weeks is the latency to gains (PDF 305); 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF 360).\" Keep the \"three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks\" reconciliation, labelled Inference, as a link between block length (a) and stage length (b). Add as Inference: \"5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (23-25% of the annual volume, PDF 305, 381), which makes the month the working unit; 'up to 2 months' reads as a ceiling.\" Add the link [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. |\n| heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance | GO | Checked: the full restorative-stage quote against the PDF 361 / p.350 image, the \"after the volume of loading is decreased\" quote (PDF 360), the 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks (PDF 305), and the 7-10 day pause (PDF 360). The Bompa & Haff quote matches the vault `allow-restoration` entry word for word. Baseline line 151 (Inferred) matches. The GPP mechanism is correctly marked as not given. The Example is labelled Inference. `supported` is right. | None. Minor: \"as quoted in the 5.3.3 entry above\" should read \"as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]\", since \"above\" means nothing in a standalone note. |\n| claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect | FIX | (1) Process note in the entry body: \"The Table 6.1 and 6.2.2 material it points to is in batch 6a's pages (PDF pp. 334-336), not checked here.\" The brief forbids review or process history. (I confirmed that 6.2.2 starts on PDF 334 and Table 6.1 is referenced on PDF 335.) (2) Why it works opens \"Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading\". That is a correct observation, but it is the writer's, presented without a label. Checked and correct: the full quote, including the italic \"the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity\" (PDF 361 image), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). The tension with the PDF 360 \"more extensive ... the longer the new functional level is maintained\" claim is accurate and labelled. `untested` is acceptable: one group, reported second-hand, with Bondarchuk's own \"strictly\" caveat. | (1) Replace the batch sentence with: \"The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-336 / printed pp. 323-325.\" (2) Prefix \"Inference:\" to the first sentence of Why it works. |\n| principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities.\" PDF 362 / p.351 (image) points only to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3, and supports the cyclic point with Zhurbinoi (1978). There is no Chapter 4 pointer on the page. (2) \"(Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" is attached to \"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\". In the source, the citation closes the *next* sentence (\"Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength ... (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\"). Checked and correct: the 6.8.2 quotes (PDF 361-362 images), the \"classification scheme of 6.5\" note (a correct catch: the scheme is in 6.7), Sargent 1926, Henry 1951, Margaria 1963, and the triple jump example. Baseline line 123 (Inferred) and line 116 (Goals 1 and 2 are alactic and aerobic) match. | (1) Rewrite as: \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF 362). Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point.\" (2) Quote both sentences with the citation where the source puts it, or write: \"strength preparedness ... plays an important role\", followed by a study note (Zhurbinoi, 1978). |\n| claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov | FIX | The title says \"Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top\". The source (PDF 362, image) says \"distance athletes\", not runners. The entry body rightly calls the sentence \"Unclear as printed\", but the title settles that ambiguity as fact (\"Add\"). Checked and correct: the full Vankov quote, the class-level expansions, the swimmer comparison, the convergence link (labelled Inference), and baseline line 101 (Explicit Q1). `supported` is right. | Change the title to: \"Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\". Keep the body's unclear-as-printed note as it is. |\n\nCounts: **GO 5, FIX 11, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Special-attention findings\n\n- **Borrowed management theory.** All three entries name whose model it is: Yerkes and Dodson for the arousal curve, Vroom through Tellier (1978) for expectancy, and Hersey and Blanchard for situational leadership. None presents the general model as the authors' finding. The `untested` ratings are right, since the book gives no evidence for any of them. The one gap is the unsourced introvert/extrovert claim (arousal entry, FIX 2).\n- **Distributed versus concentrated `contested`.** The Issurin critique is stated accurately against the vault brief (p.6 and p.11) and is aimed at the concentrated-unidirectional model. The entry should say that model is Verkhoshansky's. The figure-against-text claim compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities (FIX).\n- **Block-durations `contested`.** The rating holds: 3-4 weeks (PDF 360), 4-8 weeks (PDF 305) and \"up to 2 months\" (PDF 381) all measure concentrated block or period length. The 4-12 week figures measure something else (stage length at PDF 305 and 381, delayed-effect duration at PDF 375), and so does the 18-22 week figure (the stage needed to exhaust the CAR). The entry mixes these together (FIX).\n- **Fig 6.22.** Graph A decelerates; it is not straight. Two entries assert a contradiction with the text that does not exist, and claim a high-resolution crop check. This is the most serious error in the batch.\n- **Fig 6.23.** Every value the entries use is correct to within about 1 percentage point. The writer's panel A uncertainty is handled honestly. The panel B uncertainty about which curve dips deepest cannot be settled from the image.\n- **Baseline lines 116-117 (football energy systems).** Both are quoted accurately with the correct tags in `three-sport-groups`. Line 116 is quoted accurately in `functional-specialisation`. The source-against-baseline contrast on glycolytic share is presented fairly.\n\n## Missing (up to 5)\n\n1. **Decision-making and problem-solving (PDF 355-356 / p.344-345):** rate each problem high, medium or low on seriousness, urgency and growth; the six problem-solving steps; Maier's (1963) quality-versus-acceptance split and Roskin's four decision styles. This is the most directly usable part of 6.6 for running a department.\n2. **Management versus leadership (PDF 350, 353):** Tellier's management roles (responsibility, authority, accountability) set against leadership as influence (expertise, personality, charisma), and Oncken's four types of authority (position, personality, competence, integrity).\n3. **Blake-Mouton managerial grid and the Tannenbaum-Schmidt continuum with Likert's styles (PDF 353-355, Figs 6.17, 6.18 and 6.20):** the people-versus-production grid coordinates (9,1), (1,9), (1,1), (9,9) and (5,5). This could be one framework entry beside situational leadership.\n4. **Needs theories (PDF 352):** Maslow, Alderfer's ERG and McClelland's achievement, power and affiliation, with the authors' warning that Maslow's hierarchy \"has to be applied with circumspection\", and the transcendence and athletes note (Murphy & White, 1978).\n5. **The endurance monitoring limit (PDF 359):** \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity ... but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation.\" This is a monitoring point in its own right, and the compensatory entry mentions it only in passing.\n\n## Duplicates and reciprocal links\n\nThere are no duplicates. A grep of the vault and staging for Yerkes, Vroom, Hersey, Farfel, \"fundamental unit\", CAR, distributed loading, Bondarchuk 50% and Vankov found no entry that restates any of these 16. Suggested reciprocal links:\n\n- `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading...` and staging 6.9.5 `claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`.\n- `heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks...` and staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, plus vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` (for the PDF 375 delayed-effect duration).\n- `principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle...` and staging 6.2 `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap`.\n- `principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window` and staging 6.9.4 `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Fig 6.22 misread in `four-observations` and `compensatory-adaptation`: graph A decelerates, so remove the figure-against-text claim.\n2. `distributed-vs-concentrated`: replace the Fig 6.23 dip-versus-block comparison; name Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model in the Issurin line; correct \"not for medium-level\".\n3. `concentrated-blocks`: sort the conflict by quantity (block 3-4 / 4-8 weeks / up to 2 months versus stage 4-12 weeks, delayed effect 4-12 weeks, CAR stage 18-22 weeks).\n4. Remove the batch-6a process notes from `mesocycle` and `bondarchuk`.\n5. Label the writer's reasoning as Inference: `mesocycle` Why it works, `functional-specialisation` Chapter 4 pointer, `bondarchuk` Why it works opener, `logical-scheme` \"only route\".\n6. Small source mismatches: `three-sport-groups` \"further subdivide\", `compensatory` \"taken much further\", `vankov` title, `arousal` curve B and the unsourced personality claim, `logical-scheme` shaded boxes, `functional-specialisation` Zhurbinoi placement.\n7. Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" (I have verified the page).\n","numLines":73,"startLine":1,"totalLines":73}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"806b061f-5cec-4947-ba23-3ed18b9ba6de","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"24154901-f597-4f41-b001-deff9a1a3f09","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffp8a1o4iM2RHTnRY42K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JPWgjxB5G2Lv9B1oswTrV6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6b_applied.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5575,"cache_read_input_tokens":50323,"output_tokens":296,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5575},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":296,"cache_read_input_tokens":50323,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5575,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5575,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01JPWgjxB5G2Lv9B1oswTrV6":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6b_applied.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffp8ZnQyaPttsWLVwD82","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"3d9ebfd0-b37c-445d-9ac9-2d8307148081","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:05.763Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3d9ebfd0-b37c-445d-9ac9-2d8307148081","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d3b98ac2-d94a-4ded-8d2a-6ab7c83cd263","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JPWgjxB5G2Lv9B1oswTrV6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Fixes applied: Supertraining 6.4-6.8.2 (Ch6b), 16 entries\n2\t\n3\tFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 11 FIX rows, 7 numbered fix groups, 4 reciprocal-link suggestions, 5 missing ideas).\n4\tSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 305, 327, 347-362, 370, 371, 375 and 381 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f6b_text.txt`). I rendered and opened PDF 305, 348, 351, 358, 359, 360, 361, 362, 375 and 381 (`staging/_renders/f6b_p<N>.png`), the top of PDF 371 (`f6b_p371_top.png`), plus a 400 dpi crop of Fig 6.22 (`staging/_renders/f6b_p358_fig622_400.png`). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\n5\tScope: the 16 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`. I edited the 11 flagged entries plus two GO entries (`principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window`, for the PDF 359 locator, the Bompa wording and a review-suggested reciprocal link; `heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance`, for a dangling \"entry above\" pointer). No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (16 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 6a, 6c or 6d file, or the vault.\n6\t\n7\t## Fig 6.22 reading (decided from the image)\n8\t\n9\tGraph A (thick line, \"Fitness state\") on the 400 dpi crop of PDF 358 / p.347: it starts at about y = 660 px (Year 1 left edge), is at about 440 px at the Year 1/2 boundary, about 285 px at the Year 2/3 boundary and about 200 px at the right edge. The rise is about 225 px in Year 1, about 155 px in Year 2 and about 80 px in Year 3. At the Year 1/2 boundary A sits about 70 px above the straight chord from start to end. So A is a decelerating (concave) curve, consistent with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF 359). No peak is drawn within the three years. The review is right and the writer's \"nearly straight\" reading was wrong; both Fig 6.22 fixes were applied.\n10\t\n11\t| file | what changed |\n12\t|---|---|\n13\t| framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md | **Fix 6 + 5.** The \"(shaded box A)\" and \"(shaded box B)\" parentheticals are removed from the Fig 6.12 walk-through. A new sentence describes \"the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state\" (checked against the PDF 348 image). Why it works now opens: \"The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring.\" |\n14\t| heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md | **Fix 6.** Fig 6.15 now reads: \"one broad inverted-U ...; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve ('second wind'); and a narrow spike C at high arousal\" (checked against the PDF 351 image). Point 2 adds: \"The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim ('appears'), not as the authors' finding.\" The optional Eysenck and 1908 provenance notes were not added (they need a checked citation). |\n15\t| framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md | **Fix 6.** When NOT now says the authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF 357 / p.346), and that \"extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference\". Verified in the text layer: the call sits between the cyclic bullet and \"Finally, one should identify a third group\". |\n16\t| principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md | **Fix 5 + 4.** Why it works now reads: \"The authors' stated basis is 'modern sporting requirements' and 'common sense' (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe ... unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\" The batch 6a sentence is deleted. In its place: the book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" in 6.2 (PDF 327 / p.316, confirmed in the text layer) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit in 6.8.1 (PDF 358); \"the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording\". Link to [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] added there and in Related (review's reciprocal-link suggestion). |\n17\t| claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md | **Fix 1 + 7, missing idea 5.** \"a thick, nearly straight rising line A\" is now \"a thick rising line A ... whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\". \"a high-resolution crop\" is now \"a 400 dpi crop\". The \"Figure against text\" sentence is replaced with \"Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's 'parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time' (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years.\" All three \"text layer only\" locators for PDF 359 now read \"checked against the page image\" (I opened the page). Missing idea 5 is folded into the endurance caveat: the problem lies \"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\", with the full \"measures of aerobic and anaerobic output ... do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\" quote, plus a labelled Inference on what that means for conditioning tests. |\n18\t| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md | **Fix 1 + 6 + 7.** The PDF 359 locator now reads \"checked against the page image\". Why it works now says research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) \"has to be taken much further in its application to the development of sporting mastery, especially in endurance sports\"; until then they assume \"the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...\". When NOT: the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence and the \"graph A looks nearly straight\" clause are deleted, replaced with \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola, but no peak is drawn within the three years (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\" The Example's \"steadily rising fitness state A\" is now \"rising fitness state A\", to match. |\n19\t| principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md | **GO row, minor items applied.** PDF 359 locator now \"checked against the page image\". The Bompa comparison now reads \"sets out three outcomes of loading (an increasing, varied load, adaptation; an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation)\", checked against the vault `periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold` (\"a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau\"). Related adds the review's reciprocal link [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]]. |\n20\t| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md | **Fix 2.** (1) The dip-versus-block sentence is replaced: Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits \"concentrated in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360); the figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations; its x-axis ends at 18 weeks against the text's 18-22 (all checked on the PDF 360 and 361 images). (2) \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient ...\" (the vault Issurin brief, line 51, says the CU model is Verkhoshansky's). (3) \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\" is replaced with: devised for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image; the text layer misreads \"are\" as \"arc\"), \"so it is not required there\". I also changed the When to apply Inference, which said \"distributed loading for medium-level athletes\", to \"distributed loading when performance must hold; at medium qualification the authors say both forms work\". Reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] added in When NOT and Related. `contested` stays, for the Issurin reason. |\n21\t| heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md | **Fix 3.** The When NOT conflict paragraph is rewritten by quantity. **Concentrated block length (the conflict):** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360, given as the \"determined\" optimum), \"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks\" (PDF 305, image checked) and \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF 381, image checked). **Different quantities:** 4-12 week unidirectional *stage* (PDF 305, 381); 4-12 week LDTE duration, \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\" (PDF 375 / p.364, image checked); 1-3 week latency (PDF 305); 18-22 week stage to exhaust the CAR (PDF 360); per-quality upper limits in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. The 9-12 week reconciliation is kept as Inference, now framed as linking block length to stage length. New Inference: both 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 define concentration by one month's volume (23-25%, PDF 305 and 381, quoted), so the month is the working unit and \"up to 2 months\" reads as a ceiling. The PDF 381 \"text layer only\" locator is gone. The \"Unclear arithmetic\" note is unchanged. Related adds the time-limits link. `contested` kept (see Disagreements 1). |\n22\t| heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance.md | **GO row, minor item applied.** \"as quoted in the 5.3.3 entry above\" is now \"as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]\". |\n23\t| claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md | **Fix 4 + 5.** The batch 6a sentence is replaced with \"The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-335 / printed pp. 323-324 (6.2.2 opens on PDF p. 334; Table 6.1 is on PDF p. 335)\" (checked in the text layer; see Disagreements 3). Why it works now opens \"Inference: Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all ...\". |\n24\t| principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md | **Fix 5 + 6.** The Zhurbinoi passage is now quoted in full, both sentences, with \"(Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" where the source puts it (closing the second sentence; PDF 362 image). Why it works now reads: \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only. Inference: Chapter 4's argument ... supports the same point.\" |\n25\t| claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md | **Fix 6.** Title is now \"Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\". In When NOT, \"The distance-runner sentence\" is now \"The distance-athlete sentence ('distance athletes', not runners)\". Slug unchanged (it says \"distance\", not \"runners\"), so no rename. |\n26\t\n27\t## Missing ideas: placement\n28\t\n29\t| # | idea | placement |\n30\t|---|---|---|\n31\t| 1 | Decision-making and problem-solving (PDF 355-356) | **Not added: not applied to training.** The H/M/L rating, six steps, Maier and Roskin are framed for \"managers or committees\"; the page has no coach, athlete or training example. |\n32\t| 2 | Management versus leadership; Oncken's authority types (PDF 350, 353) | **Not added: not applied to training.** The only sport mention is an illustration of the terms (team captains lead on the field, managers off it); nothing applies it to training. |\n33\t| 3 | Blake-Mouton grid, Tannenbaum-Schmidt continuum, Likert (PDF 353-355) | **Not added: not applied to training.** The grid coordinates are given for generic management styles. The nearby \"applied by managers, leaders and coaches steeped in the traditional ways\" refers to the Fig 6.17 traditional model, as a criticism, not to the grid, and gives no training use. |\n34\t| 4 | Needs theories (PDF 352) | **Not added: not applied to training.** The one athlete note (world-class performances in altered states, Murphy & White 1978) describes transcendence; it is not applied to training. The \"applied with circumspection\" warning concerns Maslow's model in general. |\n35\t| 5 | Endurance monitoring limit (PDF 359) | **Folded into** `claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation` (its When NOT already held the endurance caveat from the same paragraph), with a labelled Inference. |\n36\t\n37\tNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt` is unchanged.\n38\t\n39\t## Reciprocal links (within 6b files only; all targets exist in the book folder)\n40\t\n41\t- distributed-vs-concentrated → [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]: added.\n42\t- concentrated-blocks → [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]: added. [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] was already in Related; it is now also cited in the conflict paragraph for the PDF 375 duration.\n43\t- mesocycle → [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]: added.\n44\t- adaptation-reserve-window → [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]]: added.\n45\t- The back-links from those 6c/6d/6a files to the 6b entries are for their own fixers or the coordinator; I did not edit them.\n46\t\n47\t## Disagreements with the review\n48\t\n49\t1. **Block lengths: `contested` kept, but the conflict is narrower than the review states.** The review calls 3-4 weeks, 4-8 weeks and \"up to 2 months\" one quantity in conflict. On the pages, 4-8 weeks (PDF 305, offered \"for instance\") and \"up to 2 months\" (PDF 381, a ceiling) agree with each other. The real conflict is 3-4 weeks, which PDF 360 presents as the determined optimum, against blocks up to twice that long in 5.3.3. The entry now says so explicitly. It still counts as a conflict on one quantity, so `contested` stays.\n50\t2. **PDF 375 adds a link the review did not mention.** PDF 375 says the LDTE duration \"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\". So the 4-12 week LDTE figure and the 4-12 week stage figure are tied by the book itself; they are still different quantities from block length. Quoted in the entry.\n51\t3. **Bondarchuk page range.** The review's correction text gives \"PDF pp. 334-336 / printed pp. 323-325\". The text layer puts the 6.2.2 heading on PDF 334 and Table 6.1 (its reference and caption) on PDF 335; nothing for either is on 336. The entry says pp. 334-335 / printed 323-324.\n52\t4. **Fig 6.22:** no disagreement. My own measurement agrees with the review (see the reading above).\n53\t5. **Optional items not applied.** Expectancy (Tellier provenance note) and situational leadership (\"Inference:\" prefix) are GO rows with optional suggestions, and arousal's Eysenck/1908 notes need a checked citation. None was applied. I did apply two minor GO-row items: the adaptation-reserve entry (the PDF 359 locator the review asked for, and the Bompa wording, verified against the vault file) and the restorative-stage pointer (a dangling \"above\" in a standalone note).\n54\t\n55\tEvery other review claim held up on the page: Fig 6.12 shaded regions (PDF 348), Fig 6.15 hump B (PDF 351), \"further subdivide\" placement (PDF 357), the \"fundamental unit\" pair (PDF 327, 358), the PDF 359 text matching the image, \"taken much further\" about research (PDF 359), Fig 6.23 dashed lines and no drawn blocks (PDF 361), the PDF 371 medium-qualification sentence, the Zhurbinoi placement and the absence of a Chapter 4 pointer (PDF 362), and \"distance athletes\" (PDF 362).\n56\t\n57\t## Checks\n58\t\n59\tGrep proof, run from the book folder (new wording, one line per change):\n60\t\n61\t```\n62\tframework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md:23:which overlap at fitness state\n63\tframework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md:31:Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route\n64\theuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md:23:a second hump B on the right-hand slope\n65\theuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md:18:The book gives no source for the personality difference\n66\tframework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md:27:extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference\n67\tprinciple--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md:25:The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\"\n68\tprinciple--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md:29:the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit\n69\tclaim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md:22:whose slope shrinks each year\n70\tclaim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md:28:Graph A flattens year by year\n71\tclaim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md:28:they do not express the internal changes\n72\tclaim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md:25:research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye)\n73\tclaim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md:29:Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola\n74\tprinciple--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md:22:an unchanging load, plateau\n75\tprinciple--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md:30:loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue\n76\tframework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md:32:Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model\n77\tframework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md:32:Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18\n78\tframework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md:32:so it is not required there\n79\theuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md:26:Contested inside the book (concentrated block length)\n80\theuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md:26:Different quantities, not part of the conflict\n81\theuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md:26:which makes the month the working unit\n82\theuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance.md:27:as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration\n83\tclaim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md:24:6.2.2 opens on PDF p. 334\n84\tclaim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md:20:Inference: Bondarchuk's study as reported\n85\tprinciple--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md:22:the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only\n86\tprinciple--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md:20:accelerate the increase in functional strength (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\n87\tclaim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md:14:for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\tOld phrases, count of 6b files still holding each (all 0):\n91\t\n92\t```\n93\tshaded box A: 0\n94\tspikes B and C: 0\n95\town call to \"further subdivide\" applies: 0\n96\tBatch 6a: 0\n97\tbatch 6a: 0\n98\tnearly straight: 0\n99\ttext layer only: 0\n100\tNot for novices or medium-level: 0\n101\tFigure against text: the figure: 0\n102\tDistance Runners Add: 0\n103\tpoint to Chapter 4: 0\n104\t5.3.3 entry above: 0\n105\thas to be taken much further,\" assuming: 0\n106\t```\n107\t\n108\t`grep -i batch` over the 16 6b files: no matches.\n109\t\n110\tMechanical greps over the 16 scoped files (run under `/bin/bash`):\n111\t\n112\t```\n113\t== em dashes not in section headers:\n114\t(end)\n115\t== header em-dash count per file:\n116\t  16 6\n117\t== wrapped wikilinks:\n118\t(end)\n119\t== CR endings:\n120\t0\n121\t```\n122\t\n123\tAll wikilink targets resolve (the four `old-bull-performance-course--old-bull/...` targets are in the vault under `performance-technology-and-leadership/`).\n124\t\n125\tLint, run from the Flint root:\n126\t\n127\t```\n128\t$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\n129\tsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\n130\tTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n131\t```\n132\t\n133\tNo 6b file appears in the lint output. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope and not edited.\n134\t"}]},"uuid":"309fd0c6-8816-4843-9758-1a1943d1e76c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:05.907Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6b_applied.md","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 6.4-6.8.2 (Ch6b), 16 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 11 FIX rows, 7 numbered fix groups, 4 reciprocal-link suggestions, 5 missing ideas).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 305, 327, 347-362, 370, 371, 375 and 381 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f6b_text.txt`). I rendered and opened PDF 305, 348, 351, 358, 359, 360, 361, 362, 375 and 381 (`staging/_renders/f6b_p<N>.png`), the top of PDF 371 (`f6b_p371_top.png`), plus a 400 dpi crop of Fig 6.22 (`staging/_renders/f6b_p358_fig622_400.png`). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 16 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`. I edited the 11 flagged entries plus two GO entries (`principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window`, for the PDF 359 locator, the Bompa wording and a review-suggested reciprocal link; `heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance`, for a dangling \"entry above\" pointer). No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (16 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 6a, 6c or 6d file, or the vault.\n\n## Fig 6.22 reading (decided from the image)\n\nGraph A (thick line, \"Fitness state\") on the 400 dpi crop of PDF 358 / p.347: it starts at about y = 660 px (Year 1 left edge), is at about 440 px at the Year 1/2 boundary, about 285 px at the Year 2/3 boundary and about 200 px at the right edge. The rise is about 225 px in Year 1, about 155 px in Year 2 and about 80 px in Year 3. At the Year 1/2 boundary A sits about 70 px above the straight chord from start to end. So A is a decelerating (concave) curve, consistent with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF 359). No peak is drawn within the three years. The review is right and the writer's \"nearly straight\" reading was wrong; both Fig 6.22 fixes were applied.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md | **Fix 6 + 5.** The \"(shaded box A)\" and \"(shaded box B)\" parentheticals are removed from the Fig 6.12 walk-through. A new sentence describes \"the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state\" (checked against the PDF 348 image). Why it works now opens: \"The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring.\" |\n| heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md | **Fix 6.** Fig 6.15 now reads: \"one broad inverted-U ...; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve ('second wind'); and a narrow spike C at high arousal\" (checked against the PDF 351 image). Point 2 adds: \"The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim ('appears'), not as the authors' finding.\" The optional Eysenck and 1908 provenance notes were not added (they need a checked citation). |\n| framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md | **Fix 6.** When NOT now says the authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF 357 / p.346), and that \"extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference\". Verified in the text layer: the call sits between the cyclic bullet and \"Finally, one should identify a third group\". |\n| principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md | **Fix 5 + 4.** Why it works now reads: \"The authors' stated basis is 'modern sporting requirements' and 'common sense' (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe ... unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\" The batch 6a sentence is deleted. In its place: the book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" in 6.2 (PDF 327 / p.316, confirmed in the text layer) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit in 6.8.1 (PDF 358); \"the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording\". Link to [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] added there and in Related (review's reciprocal-link suggestion). |\n| claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md | **Fix 1 + 7, missing idea 5.** \"a thick, nearly straight rising line A\" is now \"a thick rising line A ... whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\". \"a high-resolution crop\" is now \"a 400 dpi crop\". The \"Figure against text\" sentence is replaced with \"Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's 'parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time' (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years.\" All three \"text layer only\" locators for PDF 359 now read \"checked against the page image\" (I opened the page). Missing idea 5 is folded into the endurance caveat: the problem lies \"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\", with the full \"measures of aerobic and anaerobic output ... do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\" quote, plus a labelled Inference on what that means for conditioning tests. |\n| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md | **Fix 1 + 6 + 7.** The PDF 359 locator now reads \"checked against the page image\". Why it works now says research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) \"has to be taken much further in its application to the development of sporting mastery, especially in endurance sports\"; until then they assume \"the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...\". When NOT: the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence and the \"graph A looks nearly straight\" clause are deleted, replaced with \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola, but no peak is drawn within the three years (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\" The Example's \"steadily rising fitness state A\" is now \"rising fitness state A\", to match. |\n| principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md | **GO row, minor items applied.** PDF 359 locator now \"checked against the page image\". The Bompa comparison now reads \"sets out three outcomes of loading (an increasing, varied load, adaptation; an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation)\", checked against the vault `periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold` (\"a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau\"). Related adds the review's reciprocal link [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]]. |\n| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md | **Fix 2.** (1) The dip-versus-block sentence is replaced: Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits \"concentrated in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360); the figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations; its x-axis ends at 18 weeks against the text's 18-22 (all checked on the PDF 360 and 361 images). (2) \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient ...\" (the vault Issurin brief, line 51, says the CU model is Verkhoshansky's). (3) \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\" is replaced with: devised for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image; the text layer misreads \"are\" as \"arc\"), \"so it is not required there\". I also changed the When to apply Inference, which said \"distributed loading for medium-level athletes\", to \"distributed loading when performance must hold; at medium qualification the authors say both forms work\". Reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] added in When NOT and Related. `contested` stays, for the Issurin reason. |\n| heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md | **Fix 3.** The When NOT conflict paragraph is rewritten by quantity. **Concentrated block length (the conflict):** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360, given as the \"determined\" optimum), \"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks\" (PDF 305, image checked) and \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF 381, image checked). **Different quantities:** 4-12 week unidirectional *stage* (PDF 305, 381); 4-12 week LDTE duration, \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\" (PDF 375 / p.364, image checked); 1-3 week latency (PDF 305); 18-22 week stage to exhaust the CAR (PDF 360); per-quality upper limits in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. The 9-12 week reconciliation is kept as Inference, now framed as linking block length to stage length. New Inference: both 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 define concentration by one month's volume (23-25%, PDF 305 and 381, quoted), so the month is the working unit and \"up to 2 months\" reads as a ceiling. The PDF 381 \"text layer only\" locator is gone. The \"Unclear arithmetic\" note is unchanged. Related adds the time-limits link. `contested` kept (see Disagreements 1). |\n| heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance.md | **GO row, minor item applied.** \"as quoted in the 5.3.3 entry above\" is now \"as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]\". |\n| claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md | **Fix 4 + 5.** The batch 6a sentence is replaced with \"The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-335 / printed pp. 323-324 (6.2.2 opens on PDF p. 334; Table 6.1 is on PDF p. 335)\" (checked in the text layer; see Disagreements 3). Why it works now opens \"Inference: Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all ...\". |\n| principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md | **Fix 5 + 6.** The Zhurbinoi passage is now quoted in full, both sentences, with \"(Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" where the source puts it (closing the second sentence; PDF 362 image). Why it works now reads: \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only. Inference: Chapter 4's argument ... supports the same point.\" |\n| claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md | **Fix 6.** Title is now \"Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\". In When NOT, \"The distance-runner sentence\" is now \"The distance-athlete sentence ('distance athletes', not runners)\". Slug unchanged (it says \"distance\", not \"runners\"), so no rename. |\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Decision-making and problem-solving (PDF 355-356) | **Not added: not applied to training.** The H/M/L rating, six steps, Maier and Roskin are framed for \"managers or committees\"; the page has no coach, athlete or training example. |\n| 2 | Management versus leadership; Oncken's authority types (PDF 350, 353) | **Not added: not applied to training.** The only sport mention is an illustration of the terms (team captains lead on the field, managers off it); nothing applies it to training. |\n| 3 | Blake-Mouton grid, Tannenbaum-Schmidt continuum, Likert (PDF 353-355) | **Not added: not applied to training.** The grid coordinates are given for generic management styles. The nearby \"applied by managers, leaders and coaches steeped in the traditional ways\" refers to the Fig 6.17 traditional model, as a criticism, not to the grid, and gives no training use. |\n| 4 | Needs theories (PDF 352) | **Not added: not applied to training.** The one athlete note (world-class performances in altered states, Murphy & White 1978) describes transcendence; it is not applied to training. The \"applied with circumspection\" warning concerns Maslow's model in general. |\n| 5 | Endurance monitoring limit (PDF 359) | **Folded into** `claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation` (its When NOT already held the endurance caveat from the same paragraph), with a labelled Inference. |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Reciprocal links (within 6b files only; all targets exist in the book folder)\n\n- distributed-vs-concentrated → [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]: added.\n- concentrated-blocks → [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]: added. [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] was already in Related; it is now also cited in the conflict paragraph for the PDF 375 duration.\n- mesocycle → [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]: added.\n- adaptation-reserve-window → [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]]: added.\n- The back-links from those 6c/6d/6a files to the 6b entries are for their own fixers or the coordinator; I did not edit them.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Block lengths: `contested` kept, but the conflict is narrower than the review states.** The review calls 3-4 weeks, 4-8 weeks and \"up to 2 months\" one quantity in conflict. On the pages, 4-8 weeks (PDF 305, offered \"for instance\") and \"up to 2 months\" (PDF 381, a ceiling) agree with each other. The real conflict is 3-4 weeks, which PDF 360 presents as the determined optimum, against blocks up to twice that long in 5.3.3. The entry now says so explicitly. It still counts as a conflict on one quantity, so `contested` stays.\n2. **PDF 375 adds a link the review did not mention.** PDF 375 says the LDTE duration \"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\". So the 4-12 week LDTE figure and the 4-12 week stage figure are tied by the book itself; they are still different quantities from block length. Quoted in the entry.\n3. **Bondarchuk page range.** The review's correction text gives \"PDF pp. 334-336 / printed pp. 323-325\". The text layer puts the 6.2.2 heading on PDF 334 and Table 6.1 (its reference and caption) on PDF 335; nothing for either is on 336. The entry says pp. 334-335 / printed 323-324.\n4. **Fig 6.22:** no disagreement. My own measurement agrees with the review (see the reading above).\n5. **Optional items not applied.** Expectancy (Tellier provenance note) and situational leadership (\"Inference:\" prefix) are GO rows with optional suggestions, and arousal's Eysenck/1908 notes need a checked citation. None was applied. I did apply two minor GO-row items: the adaptation-reserve entry (the PDF 359 locator the review asked for, and the Bompa wording, verified against the vault file) and the restorative-stage pointer (a dangling \"above\" in a standalone note).\n\nEvery other review claim held up on the page: Fig 6.12 shaded regions (PDF 348), Fig 6.15 hump B (PDF 351), \"further subdivide\" placement (PDF 357), the \"fundamental unit\" pair (PDF 327, 358), the PDF 359 text matching the image, \"taken much further\" about research (PDF 359), Fig 6.23 dashed lines and no drawn blocks (PDF 361), the PDF 371 medium-qualification sentence, the Zhurbinoi placement and the absence of a Chapter 4 pointer (PDF 362), and \"distance athletes\" (PDF 362).\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder (new wording, one line per change):\n\n```\nframework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md:23:which overlap at fitness state\nframework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md:31:Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route\nheuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md:23:a second hump B on the right-hand slope\nheuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md:18:The book gives no source for the personality difference\nframework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md:27:extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference\nprinciple--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md:25:The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\"\nprinciple--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md:29:the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit\nclaim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md:22:whose slope shrinks each year\nclaim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md:28:Graph A flattens year by year\nclaim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md:28:they do not express the internal changes\nclaim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md:25:research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye)\nclaim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md:29:Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola\nprinciple--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md:22:an unchanging load, plateau\nprinciple--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md:30:loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue\nframework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md:32:Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model\nframework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md:32:Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18\nframework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md:32:so it is not required there\nheuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md:26:Contested inside the book (concentrated block length)\nheuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md:26:Different quantities, not part of the conflict\nheuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md:26:which makes the month the working unit\nheuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance.md:27:as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration\nclaim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md:24:6.2.2 opens on PDF p. 334\nclaim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md:20:Inference: Bondarchuk's study as reported\nprinciple--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md:22:the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only\nprinciple--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md:20:accelerate the increase in functional strength (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\nclaim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md:14:for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic\n```\n\nOld phrases, count of 6b files still holding each (all 0):\n\n```\nshaded box A: 0\nspikes B and C: 0\nown call to \"further subdivide\" applies: 0\nBatch 6a: 0\nbatch 6a: 0\nnearly straight: 0\ntext layer only: 0\nNot for novices or medium-level: 0\nFigure against text: the figure: 0\nDistance Runners Add: 0\npoint to Chapter 4: 0\n5.3.3 entry above: 0\nhas to be taken much further,\" assuming: 0\n```\n\n`grep -i batch` over the 16 6b files: no matches.\n\nMechanical greps over the 16 scoped files (run under `/bin/bash`):\n\n```\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== header em-dash count per file:\n  16 6\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== CR endings:\n0\n```\n\nAll wikilink targets resolve (the four `old-bull-performance-course--old-bull/...` targets are in the vault under `performance-technology-and-leadership/`).\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nNo 6b file appears in the lint output. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope and not edited.\n","numLines":134,"startLine":1,"totalLines":134}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3d9ebfd0-b37c-445d-9ac9-2d8307148081","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
