on-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; cat \"$f\"; done","description":"Print first four FIX entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffp9YtcWRfZ1DUk99BsX","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b01a38de-9267-4d13-ba29-4928321dcb39","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:19.491Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b01a38de-9267-4d13-ba29-4928321dcb39","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d3b98ac2-d94a-4ded-8d2a-6ab7c83cd263","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nen4MQwZNZQnZMkX6SDbxT","type":"tool_result","content":"=== framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.4\"\ntopic: training as a management system\ncategory: [programming, monitoring, training-load, theory]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Training as a Managed System: Training Load, Fitness State and External Interactions, Controlled at Two Levels\n\n**What it is** — The authors treat programming \"as a problem in management\" and name three components of the managed system (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336, checked against the page image):\n1. \"The complex of the athlete's external influences encountered in competition.\" \"The greater the athlete's motor potential, the more effectively the complex of external interactions can be organised.\" Sports results \"are the observable product of the organisation of the athlete's external interactions.\"\n2. \"the athlete's fitness state,\" defined as \"the athlete's structural-functional qualities which determine the athlete's potential to produce sporting prowess (Zatsiorsky, 1979).\"\n3. \"specialised motor activities, i.e. the training load.\"\n\nThe causal chain runs one way: \"The dosage of the training load is prescribed to promote changes in the athlete's fitness state which, in turn, entails changes in the complex of external interactions of the athlete and a corresponding improvement in sporting results.\" When planned results are reached, \"the sequence of the preceding conditions is repeated at higher levels\" (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336).\n\nFig 6.12, \"A logical scheme for organising sports training (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337, checked against the page image), draws the loop: Fitness needs for given sport, Training objectives, Programming of training, Characteristics of training model, then Training load, Training potential of loading, Athlete's fitness state, Training effect of loading, Athlete's external interactions, Change in performance, and Assessment of effect (fed by \"Sports Competition\" and \"Laboratory Tests\"), which returns to Fitness needs. Two shaded areas mark the subsystems: the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state.\n\nKey terms (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337):\n- **Training potential of the loading:** \"its potential for promoting adaptation of the body and corresponding changes in its fitness state,\" and \"a relative concept which should be examined and assessed in accordance with the athlete's current fitness state.\"\n- **Assessment of the training effect:** \"measuring the performance change that the loading produces in each case under competitive and laboratory conditions.\"\n- **Two levels of control:** the fitness state (\"control of the effect of the loading on fitness\") and external interactions (\"control of the change in their characteristics as a result of changes in fitness\").\n- **Two subsystems:** A = {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}; B = {Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State, Athlete's External Interactions}. \"the lower the measures for the first one (A), the weaker the link in the entire management system.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring. The basis for the training effect is \"the accumulation of adaptive traces within the body produced by the entire complex of training influences\" (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337). They give no data for the scheme; it is a conceptual model, hence `untested`.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when auditing a monitoring set-up, for each test, ask which box of Fig 6.12 it measures. A jump test reads the fitness state; game or practice output reads external interactions; neither alone closes the loop. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"fitness state\" corresponds to that preparedness, and the two-level control is the same split between what the body can do and what it shows in competition. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: Fig 6.12 gives each test a place to answer to: a test that sits in no box of the loop is the kind the rule removes.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme names what to measure but gives no measures, thresholds or numbers. \"One can be certain that the lower the measures for the first one (A), the weaker the link\" is stated without a study. The authors also widen \"management\" in the same passage to \"motivation, the athlete's personality and social factors\" (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336), which the figure does not show. Inference: the model is linear and single-athlete; it does not cover fatigue, injury or sport-skill learning as separate inputs.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a sprinter's training load (sprints, jumps, lifts) is assessed at level A by laboratory tests (jump height, isometric force) and at level B by race times; if the tests rise but race times do not, the weak link is between fitness state and external interactions, not the load.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation]] (6.5, the research programme behind Fig 6.13), [[old-bull-performance-course--old-bull/framework--monitoring-versus-testing]] (Weber separates short-horizon monitoring from phase-length testing; Inference: a practical split for the two control levels)\n=== heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.6.1\"\ntopic: arousal, stress and restoration\ncategory: [psychology, restoration, overtraining, individualisation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Arousal Optimum Is Individual: Fit Restoration to Personality and Task Complexity, and Do Not Read the Yerkes-Dodson Curve Literally\n\n**What it is** — The model is Yerkes and Dodson's inverted-U of performance against arousal; the authors borrow it and apply it to training stress and restoration. The 6.6.1 section cites \"(Siff, 1990)\" as the source of its selection of models; this entry still writes \"the authors\" because no passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person. The authors' points (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340, checked against the page image, unless noted):\n1. **The optimum is individual.** \"There appears to be an optimum level of arousal for any person performing a specific task at a specific time.\" The curve's shape \"depends on individual characteristics such as level of introversion/extroversion, level of exhaustion, standard of health, and task complexity,\" and \"all motivational techniques are not universally applicable to everyone.\"\n2. **Personality and restoration.** \"the introvert appears to have a lower optimal arousal zone, unlike the extrovert who requires greater stimulation and can cope better with high levels of stress.\" This \"has important implications for the prescription of restorative procedures, with the introvert probably requiring early restoration and the extrovert requiring careful monitoring to prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\" The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim (\"appears\"), not as the authors' finding.\n3. **Task complexity.** \"Complex tasks require low levels of arousal to reach optimal performance over very limited periods (e.g. in golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting), whereas simple sports tasks may be executed over a longer period at higher levels of arousal.\" Applied to training: \"Excessive exposure of an athlete to complex training tasks can lead rapidly to 'overstress', while simple tasks may be insufficiently challenging to produce continued supercompensation. A balance between complexity, simplicity, intensity and volume of load is necessary to ensure growth and to minimise overtraining\" (PDF pp. 351-352 / printed pp. 340-341).\n4. **Repeated stress changes the response.** \"regular exposure to a stressor may produce either habituation or oversensitisation, so that the same level of stress may not produce the same response at a later stage. In either event, timely use of restoration measures is of central importance.\"\n5. **Limits of the curve.** It \"implies that there is just one discrete peak\"; the \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B) and the effect of task complexity on curve shape are two of several reasons why the original curve must be applied to restoration and stress management with circumspection.\" Also, \"it is well known that both sensory underload and overload may lead to phases of enhanced performance (curves A, C),\" and meditation and similar states \"may enhance performance in certain situations (curve A).\" Hence \"both optimisation and transcendental stress management strategies are necessary.\"\n\nFig 6.15 (checked against the page image) is a schematic with no units: one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve (\"second wind\"); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the introvert/extrovert or task-complexity claims; point 2 is hedged (\"appears\", \"probably\"), and the \"it is well known\" sentence in point 5 carries no citation.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when an athlete's response to the same load differs from a teammate's: consider temperament and task complexity before changing the load. Technical sessions (high complexity) may need calm conditions and short exposures; simple conditioning can tolerate higher arousal for longer. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the authors' point 2 suggests that load design alone may under-serve the athletes who need restoration early, and point 4 says the same load can stop meaning the same thing over a season.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: do not sort athletes into introvert and extrovert recovery plans on this basis, since the authors give no study and their own wording is tentative. Inference: a measured fatigue marker is the better trigger for restoration. The figure's curves A, B and C are illustrations, not data. The task-complexity claim is about arousal during performance; the jump to \"complex training tasks ... 'overstress'\" is the authors' extension, also without data.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting as complex tasks needing low arousal over very limited periods (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340). Inference: a pole vaulter's technical session is kept short and calm, while the same athlete's sprint conditioning tolerates a louder, longer session.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]], [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[claim--voluntary-involuntary-strength-reserve-magnitudes]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability]]\n=== framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.7\"\ntopic: classification of sports\ncategory: [classification, energy-systems, programming, specificity]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classify Sports by Movement Organisation and Energy Supply: Acyclic, Cyclic and Complex (Farfel 1969)\n\n**What it is** — Programming rules need \"the limits of generality and specificity in individual sports or groups of related sports,\" so sports must be classified \"scientifically.\" \"Classification of sports into individual, team, ball or water sports, for example, is not scientifically useful; it is more appropriate to classify sports on the basis of the particular organisation of the sporting movements and characteristics of the physical systems used to produce their working effect\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345, checked against the page image). The authors adopt three groups \"as already outlined by Farfel (1969)\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346, checked against the page image):\n1. **Acyclic sports**, \"in which perfecting of the neuromuscular system for precise regulation of movements and the ability to produce great power play the dominant roles.\" Earlier on the same page: \"complex organisation of motor actions and an intense concentration of working effort, displayed briefly,\" with energy \"primarily through ATP-CP energy processes.\" Subgroups: \"powerful, brief, explosive efforts\" and \"fine motor control and spatially precise movement.\"\n2. **Cyclic sports** \"(usually of submaximal and of moderate power), associated primarily with the production of muscular work through aerobic processes\": \"prolonged repetition of stereotyped work cycles of relatively simple organisation which do not require maximal muscular tension.\" Subgroups: \"extensive oxygen utilisation\" and work where \"the muscles have an inadequate oxygen supply.\"\n3. **Complex (combined) sports**, \"where characteristically there is a high variability of motor actions under conditions of advancing fatigue and varying intensity of work, typified by team sports and one-on-one events (e.g. boxing and fencing).\" Their energy production \"is of a mixed aerobic-anaerobic character, with the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions.\"\n\nA second rule: \"In classifying sports, it is necessary to consider competitive, as well as training activities,\" because training exercises can differ from competition, \"often due to the necessity for repeated reproduction of the competitive movements in training\"; this is where \"special endurance (e.g. speed-, jumping-, and strength-endurance)\" belongs. The authors' example: the 100 m takes about 10 s and \"does not depend on oxidative efficiency. However, in order for a sprinter to prepare himself to effectively display such effort, he should execute training loads that develop his aerobic and anaerobic potential, in particular the latter\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is physiological: \"The more prolonged and the lower the intensity of muscular work, the greater the role of aerobic processes\"; the glycolytic role \"grows as the duration of work decreases and the power of muscular work increases\"; in brief acyclic work under heavy load, athletes overcome resistance in \"tenths and even hundredths of a second,\" which demands \"an effective and reliable biodynamic structure\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345). The three-group scheme itself is Farfel's convention, adopted without data, hence `supported` rather than `proven`.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: use it as the first question when writing a programme for a new sport or position: which group, and which subgroup? The authors use this scheme again in 6.8.2 to say which systems specialise (see Related). Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\"; \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 115, Explicit). Inference: Farfel's grouping is a coarse first pass at that priority choice, and the sprinter example supports training activities that differ from the competition's own energy profile.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Source against Eric's baseline on football: the authors put team sports in the complex group, with \"the greater part\" of energy \"derived from glycolytic reactions\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning \"is treated as a 'worst case scenario' to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1), and \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)). The authors give no football data, and \"team sports\" is a broad category. Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough (a lineman's short bursts against a receiver's repeated sprints) that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference. This is a different scheme from Diachkov's four categories by motor regime used in 5.1 (see Related).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: boxing and fencing as one-on-one complex sports; the 100 m sprinter whose event does not depend on oxidative efficiency but whose training should still develop aerobic and, especially, anaerobic potential (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (Diachkov's four sport categories, 5.1; a different classification), [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]] (6.8.2 applies this scheme), [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]], [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]], [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]]\n=== principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: mesocycle as the unit of training\ncategory: [periodisation, programming, supercompensation, planning]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plan in Mesocycles, Not Microcycles, and Do Not Read Every Planned Dip in Work Capacity as a Programming Error\n\n**What it is** — The authors list three practical rules that followed from supercompensation research (Weigert's law, \"first formally described by Folbrot (1941, 1952, 1958)\"; also Yakovlev 1955, 1975; Gorkin et al 1973; Volkov 1975; Monogarov & Platonov 1975; Sergeyev 1981) (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n- \"It is necessary for the athlete to maintain a state of adaptation during training, so that it is possible to execute the fundamental sporting actions effectively over an extended period\"\n- \"Repetition of training loads when the body is in a state of insufficient restoration is generally undesirable, although this is permissible in certain instances\"\n- \"Special work capacity should rise steadily during the course of training; any decrease at given stages of training would indicate that the training is incorrectly organised.\"\n\nTheir verdict: \"Undoubtedly, all of these premises were progressive in their time and to a certain extent are still valid today. However, modern sporting requirements reveal the necessity to alter the main approach to structuring training and obey common sense, which indicates that the fundamental unit of training should be the mesocycle and not the microcycle.\" This requires \"An extensive study of the characteristics of long-term adaptation,\" whose value \"will be appreciated only if one can observe the specific effect of given training loads on the dynamics of the athlete's state and determine the quantitative characteristics of the adaptation process\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\nThe same section then describes a loading form that deliberately lowers the functional indicators for weeks (concentrated loading, Fig 6.23; PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350). Inference: this is the authors' own revision of the third rule: a planned decrease inside a concentrated mesocycle is not, in their model, a sign of incorrect organisation.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks), [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] (6.2, the session as \"the fundamental unit\")","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"55dce65b-1a68-4c88-b223-77925f502541","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:19.704Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.4\"\ntopic: training as a management system\ncategory: [programming, monitoring, training-load, theory]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Training as a Managed System: Training Load, Fitness State and External Interactions, Controlled at Two Levels\n\n**What it is** — The authors treat programming \"as a problem in management\" and name three components of the managed system (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336, checked against the page image):\n1. \"The complex of the athlete's external influences encountered in competition.\" \"The greater the athlete's motor potential, the more effectively the complex of external interactions can be organised.\" Sports results \"are the observable product of the organisation of the athlete's external interactions.\"\n2. \"the athlete's fitness state,\" defined as \"the athlete's structural-functional qualities which determine the athlete's potential to produce sporting prowess (Zatsiorsky, 1979).\"\n3. \"specialised motor activities, i.e. the training load.\"\n\nThe causal chain runs one way: \"The dosage of the training load is prescribed to promote changes in the athlete's fitness state which, in turn, entails changes in the complex of external interactions of the athlete and a corresponding improvement in sporting results.\" When planned results are reached, \"the sequence of the preceding conditions is repeated at higher levels\" (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336).\n\nFig 6.12, \"A logical scheme for organising sports training (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337, checked against the page image), draws the loop: Fitness needs for given sport, Training objectives, Programming of training, Characteristics of training model, then Training load, Training potential of loading, Athlete's fitness state, Training effect of loading, Athlete's external interactions, Change in performance, and Assessment of effect (fed by \"Sports Competition\" and \"Laboratory Tests\"), which returns to Fitness needs. Two shaded areas mark the subsystems: the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state.\n\nKey terms (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337):\n- **Training potential of the loading:** \"its potential for promoting adaptation of the body and corresponding changes in its fitness state,\" and \"a relative concept which should be examined and assessed in accordance with the athlete's current fitness state.\"\n- **Assessment of the training effect:** \"measuring the performance change that the loading produces in each case under competitive and laboratory conditions.\"\n- **Two levels of control:** the fitness state (\"control of the effect of the loading on fitness\") and external interactions (\"control of the change in their characteristics as a result of changes in fitness\").\n- **Two subsystems:** A = {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}; B = {Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State, Athlete's External Interactions}. \"the lower the measures for the first one (A), the weaker the link in the entire management system.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring. The basis for the training effect is \"the accumulation of adaptive traces within the body produced by the entire complex of training influences\" (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337). They give no data for the scheme; it is a conceptual model, hence `untested`.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when auditing a monitoring set-up, for each test, ask which box of Fig 6.12 it measures. A jump test reads the fitness state; game or practice output reads external interactions; neither alone closes the loop. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"fitness state\" corresponds to that preparedness, and the two-level control is the same split between what the body can do and what it shows in competition. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: Fig 6.12 gives each test a place to answer to: a test that sits in no box of the loop is the kind the rule removes.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme names what to measure but gives no measures, thresholds or numbers. \"One can be certain that the lower the measures for the first one (A), the weaker the link\" is stated without a study. The authors also widen \"management\" in the same passage to \"motivation, the athlete's personality and social factors\" (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336), which the figure does not show. Inference: the model is linear and single-athlete; it does not cover fatigue, injury or sport-skill learning as separate inputs.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a sprinter's training load (sprints, jumps, lifts) is assessed at level A by laboratory tests (jump height, isometric force) and at level B by race times; if the tests rise but race times do not, the weak link is between fitness state and external interactions, not the load.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation]] (6.5, the research programme behind Fig 6.13), [[old-bull-performance-course--old-bull/framework--monitoring-versus-testing]] (Weber separates short-horizon monitoring from phase-length testing; Inference: a practical split for the two control levels)\n=== heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.6.1\"\ntopic: arousal, stress and restoration\ncategory: [psychology, restoration, overtraining, individualisation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Arousal Optimum Is Individual: Fit Restoration to Personality and Task Complexity, and Do Not Read the Yerkes-Dodson Curve Literally\n\n**What it is** — The model is Yerkes and Dodson's inverted-U of performance against arousal; the authors borrow it and apply it to training stress and restoration. The 6.6.1 section cites \"(Siff, 1990)\" as the source of its selection of models; this entry still writes \"the authors\" because no passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person. The authors' points (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340, checked against the page image, unless noted):\n1. **The optimum is individual.** \"There appears to be an optimum level of arousal for any person performing a specific task at a specific time.\" The curve's shape \"depends on individual characteristics such as level of introversion/extroversion, level of exhaustion, standard of health, and task complexity,\" and \"all motivational techniques are not universally applicable to everyone.\"\n2. **Personality and restoration.** \"the introvert appears to have a lower optimal arousal zone, unlike the extrovert who requires greater stimulation and can cope better with high levels of stress.\" This \"has important implications for the prescription of restorative procedures, with the introvert probably requiring early restoration and the extrovert requiring careful monitoring to prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\" The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim (\"appears\"), not as the authors' finding.\n3. **Task complexity.** \"Complex tasks require low levels of arousal to reach optimal performance over very limited periods (e.g. in golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting), whereas simple sports tasks may be executed over a longer period at higher levels of arousal.\" Applied to training: \"Excessive exposure of an athlete to complex training tasks can lead rapidly to 'overstress', while simple tasks may be insufficiently challenging to produce continued supercompensation. A balance between complexity, simplicity, intensity and volume of load is necessary to ensure growth and to minimise overtraining\" (PDF pp. 351-352 / printed pp. 340-341).\n4. **Repeated stress changes the response.** \"regular exposure to a stressor may produce either habituation or oversensitisation, so that the same level of stress may not produce the same response at a later stage. In either event, timely use of restoration measures is of central importance.\"\n5. **Limits of the curve.** It \"implies that there is just one discrete peak\"; the \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B) and the effect of task complexity on curve shape are two of several reasons why the original curve must be applied to restoration and stress management with circumspection.\" Also, \"it is well known that both sensory underload and overload may lead to phases of enhanced performance (curves A, C),\" and meditation and similar states \"may enhance performance in certain situations (curve A).\" Hence \"both optimisation and transcendental stress management strategies are necessary.\"\n\nFig 6.15 (checked against the page image) is a schematic with no units: one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve (\"second wind\"); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the introvert/extrovert or task-complexity claims; point 2 is hedged (\"appears\", \"probably\"), and the \"it is well known\" sentence in point 5 carries no citation.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when an athlete's response to the same load differs from a teammate's: consider temperament and task complexity before changing the load. Technical sessions (high complexity) may need calm conditions and short exposures; simple conditioning can tolerate higher arousal for longer. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the authors' point 2 suggests that load design alone may under-serve the athletes who need restoration early, and point 4 says the same load can stop meaning the same thing over a season.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: do not sort athletes into introvert and extrovert recovery plans on this basis, since the authors give no study and their own wording is tentative. Inference: a measured fatigue marker is the better trigger for restoration. The figure's curves A, B and C are illustrations, not data. The task-complexity claim is about arousal during performance; the jump to \"complex training tasks ... 'overstress'\" is the authors' extension, also without data.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting as complex tasks needing low arousal over very limited periods (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340). Inference: a pole vaulter's technical session is kept short and calm, while the same athlete's sprint conditioning tolerates a louder, longer session.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]], [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[claim--voluntary-involuntary-strength-reserve-magnitudes]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability]]\n=== framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.7\"\ntopic: classification of sports\ncategory: [classification, energy-systems, programming, specificity]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classify Sports by Movement Organisation and Energy Supply: Acyclic, Cyclic and Complex (Farfel 1969)\n\n**What it is** — Programming rules need \"the limits of generality and specificity in individual sports or groups of related sports,\" so sports must be classified \"scientifically.\" \"Classification of sports into individual, team, ball or water sports, for example, is not scientifically useful; it is more appropriate to classify sports on the basis of the particular organisation of the sporting movements and characteristics of the physical systems used to produce their working effect\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345, checked against the page image). The authors adopt three groups \"as already outlined by Farfel (1969)\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346, checked against the page image):\n1. **Acyclic sports**, \"in which perfecting of the neuromuscular system for precise regulation of movements and the ability to produce great power play the dominant roles.\" Earlier on the same page: \"complex organisation of motor actions and an intense concentration of working effort, displayed briefly,\" with energy \"primarily through ATP-CP energy processes.\" Subgroups: \"powerful, brief, explosive efforts\" and \"fine motor control and spatially precise movement.\"\n2. **Cyclic sports** \"(usually of submaximal and of moderate power), associated primarily with the production of muscular work through aerobic processes\": \"prolonged repetition of stereotyped work cycles of relatively simple organisation which do not require maximal muscular tension.\" Subgroups: \"extensive oxygen utilisation\" and work where \"the muscles have an inadequate oxygen supply.\"\n3. **Complex (combined) sports**, \"where characteristically there is a high variability of motor actions under conditions of advancing fatigue and varying intensity of work, typified by team sports and one-on-one events (e.g. boxing and fencing).\" Their energy production \"is of a mixed aerobic-anaerobic character, with the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions.\"\n\nA second rule: \"In classifying sports, it is necessary to consider competitive, as well as training activities,\" because training exercises can differ from competition, \"often due to the necessity for repeated reproduction of the competitive movements in training\"; this is where \"special endurance (e.g. speed-, jumping-, and strength-endurance)\" belongs. The authors' example: the 100 m takes about 10 s and \"does not depend on oxidative efficiency. However, in order for a sprinter to prepare himself to effectively display such effort, he should execute training loads that develop his aerobic and anaerobic potential, in particular the latter\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is physiological: \"The more prolonged and the lower the intensity of muscular work, the greater the role of aerobic processes\"; the glycolytic role \"grows as the duration of work decreases and the power of muscular work increases\"; in brief acyclic work under heavy load, athletes overcome resistance in \"tenths and even hundredths of a second,\" which demands \"an effective and reliable biodynamic structure\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345). The three-group scheme itself is Farfel's convention, adopted without data, hence `supported` rather than `proven`.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: use it as the first question when writing a programme for a new sport or position: which group, and which subgroup? The authors use this scheme again in 6.8.2 to say which systems specialise (see Related). Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\"; \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 115, Explicit). Inference: Farfel's grouping is a coarse first pass at that priority choice, and the sprinter example supports training activities that differ from the competition's own energy profile.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Source against Eric's baseline on football: the authors put team sports in the complex group, with \"the greater part\" of energy \"derived from glycolytic reactions\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning \"is treated as a 'worst case scenario' to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1), and \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)). The authors give no football data, and \"team sports\" is a broad category. Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough (a lineman's short bursts against a receiver's repeated sprints) that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference. This is a different scheme from Diachkov's four categories by motor regime used in 5.1 (see Related).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: boxing and fencing as one-on-one complex sports; the 100 m sprinter whose event does not depend on oxidative efficiency but whose training should still develop aerobic and, especially, anaerobic potential (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (Diachkov's four sport categories, 5.1; a different classification), [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]] (6.8.2 applies this scheme), [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]], [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]], [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]]\n=== principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: mesocycle as the unit of training\ncategory: [periodisation, programming, supercompensation, planning]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plan in Mesocycles, Not Microcycles, and Do Not Read Every Planned Dip in Work Capacity as a Programming Error\n\n**What it is** — The authors list three practical rules that followed from supercompensation research (Weigert's law, \"first formally described by Folbrot (1941, 1952, 1958)\"; also Yakovlev 1955, 1975; Gorkin et al 1973; Volkov 1975; Monogarov & Platonov 1975; Sergeyev 1981) (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n- \"It is necessary for the athlete to maintain a state of adaptation during training, so that it is possible to execute the fundamental sporting actions effectively over an extended period\"\n- \"Repetition of training loads when the body is in a state of insufficient restoration is generally undesirable, although this is permissible in certain instances\"\n- \"Special work capacity should rise steadily during the course of training; any decrease at given stages of training would indicate that the training is incorrectly organised.\"\n\nTheir verdict: \"Undoubtedly, all of these premises were progressive in their time and to a certain extent are still valid today. However, modern sporting requirements reveal the necessity to alter the main approach to structuring training and obey common sense, which indicates that the fundamental unit of training should be the mesocycle and not the microcycle.\" This requires \"An extensive study of the characteristics of long-term adaptation,\" whose value \"will be appreciated only if one can observe the specific effect of given training loads on the dynamics of the athlete's state and determine the quantitative characteristics of the adaptation process\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\nThe same section then describes a loading form that deliberately lowers the functional indicators for weeks (concentrated loading, Fig 6.23; PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350). Inference: this is the authors' own revision of the third rule: a planned decrease inside a concentrated mesocycle is not, in their model, a sign of incorrect organisation.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks), [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] (6.2, the session as \"the fundamental unit\")","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b01a38de-9267-4d13-ba29-4928321dcb39","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
