{"parentUuid":"546c560d-55ae-46eb-a4d7-42dbff41976a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffp9nxCYgkpU4pD2uSgd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Cqv2uqNvXAwSFMJjNMyqNw","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '13,$p' \"$f\"; done","description":"Print next four FIX entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8408,"cache_read_input_tokens":82777,"output_tokens":579,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":287},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8408},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":579,"cache_read_input_tokens":82777,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8408,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8408,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Cqv2uqNvXAwSFMJjNMyqNw":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '13,$p' \"$f\"; done","description":"Print next four FIX entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffp9nfLcoffFNYk7FXTL","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5bee2596-4e6e-4ea9-85ed-f08142dc8cba","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:24.674Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5bee2596-4e6e-4ea9-85ed-f08142dc8cba","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d3b98ac2-d94a-4ded-8d2a-6ab7c83cd263","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cqv2uqNvXAwSFMJjNMyqNw","type":"tool_result","content":"=== claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md\n\n# Long-Term Speed-Strength Adaptation: Rises Every Year, Starts Each Year Below Last Year's Peak, Rises Less in the Highly Qualified, and Follows the Season's Peaks\n\n**What it is** — From \"observing the dynamics of an athlete's special physical preparedness indicators over many years of training,\" the authors state four conclusions, mainly for \"sports which require explosive effort\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n1. \"The absolute and mean level of special strength preparedness increases annually.\"\n2. \"The initial level of speed-strength in each annual cycle is lower than the level at the end of the preceding year's, but higher than at its beginning.\"\n3. \"The rise in speed-strength from year to year is larger for athletes of medium qualification than for highly qualified athletes.\"\n4. The annual trend \"is determined by the traditional periodisation of training for the given sport and competition calendar. One can clearly trace a twin peak in the dynamics of speed-strength with two competition stages, because the level of special strength preparedness is higher in the second competition stage than in the first. In types of sports with one competition season, speed-strength rises regularly and uniformly.\"\n\nFig 6.22, \"The dynamics of adaptation in sports training over several years (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a 400 dpi crop), is a schematic over Years 1-3 with no numeric axis (\"Relative levels\"): a thick rising line A labelled \"Fitness state\" whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3); two wavy lines B and C labelled \"Functional indicators\" that oscillate around A and dip near each year boundary; and a lower, slowly rising wavy line D labelled \"Skill.\" The text assigns B to bi-cyclic and C to mono-cyclic periodisation, and A to \"relatively stable, long-term adaptation over many years.\" D shows that \"adaptation in sport consists not only of increased motor potential, but also the improvement of the athlete's skills to more effectively utilise this potential\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors read the annual waves (B, C) as \"temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible\" compensatory changes riding on slower stable adaptation (A) (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image; see [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]]). No athletes, sports, sample sizes or citations are given for the four conclusions; \"Extensive research allows us to reach the following conclusions\" is the only basis stated, hence `supported` at most.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: set each year's expectations from these patterns: speed-strength at the start of a new year should sit below last year's best but above last year's start; a smaller annual gain in an advanced athlete is expected, not a failure. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: Fig 6.22's A and B/C lines draw that distinction, on a season scale rather than a daily one. Eric's baseline: in football \"you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: football's one long season is closest to the authors' single-season case, where speed-strength \"rises regularly and uniformly.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The conclusions come from explosive-strength sports; the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly, and that the problem lies \"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\": \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity (i.e. endurance), but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Inference: an endurance or conditioning test can show that work capacity changed without showing which adaptation produced the change. Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years. Inference: for an athlete whose speed-strength has plateaued across years, conclusion 1 no longer describes them; that is the limit the parabola refers to.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the \"twin peak\" in speed-strength with two competition stages, the second higher than the first (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]], [[principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors]], [[framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]\n=== claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md\n\n# Compensatory Adaptation Is Temporary and Comes First; Stable Adaptation Builds on It, and Has a Limit\n\n**What it is** — The authors split adaptation into two layers (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image; the figure it refers to is on PDF p. 358):\n- **Compensatory adaptation.** The annual changes in functional indicators (Fig 6.22, graphs B and C) \"are temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible.\" Their basis is \"compensatory mechanisms, i.e. reactions designed to maintain the necessary level of functioning for brief periods under demanding conditions which ultimately lead to stable adaptation.\" These \"gradually fade as adaptation takes place,\" and \"Insofar as these mechanisms precede adaptation, they should be designated pre-adaptation phenomena (Kuznyetsov, 1979).\"\n- **Long-term, stable adaptation** (graph A). \"compensatory adaptation is a prerequisite condition for the development of long-term, stable adaptation,\" which \"can progressively increase if the functional reconstruction during the course of compensatory adaptation is sufficient to produce a noticeable effect on the external interactions of the body and increase its special work capacity.\"\n\nThree further statements on the same page:\n1. \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process. Adaptation is the dynamic maintenance of a definite level of imbalance between the body and the environment\" (Bauer, 1935; Bernstein, 1966).\n2. The systems involved improve at different rates. This heterochronicity \"is determined by: the action of particular physiological systems in establishing long-term adaptation of a specific emphasis; differences in their reactiveness (or adaptive inertia); changes in the role of these systems at different stages of long-term adaptation.\"\n3. \"Long-term compensatory adaptation cannot continue indefinitely.\" Its effect \"diminishes with each repetition,\" described \"by a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time (Fig 6.22, graph A). This indicates that an athlete's general adaptation reserves have a limit that is genetically predetermined.\" The limit shows as \"waves in the dynamics of special work capacity (graphs B and C),\" caused partly by \"traditional periodisation\" but \"a more important reason is the exhaustion of the so-called current adaptation reserves (CAR) of the body.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' account is the one above: short-term compensations hold function under stress, and if they change performance enough, the body consolidates them. They state that the picture is clearest in explosive-strength sports, and that research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) \"has to be taken much further in its application to the development of sporting mastery, especially in endurance sports\"; until then they assume \"the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms, although at another level of the physiological systems\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348). Inference: the \"genetically predetermined\" limit is inferred from the shape of the curve, not from genetic data.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: treat a season's gains in functional indicators as provisional until they hold through a lower-load period; a quality that survives the transition phase has become stable adaptation in the authors' terms. When a block stops producing gains, the authors' model points to exhausted current reserves before it points to a genetic ceiling (see [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — No data are given for the parabola or the genetic limit. Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola, but no peak is drawn within the three years (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347). The \"imbalance\" definition is a theoretical position (Bauer, Bernstein), not a measured one. Inference: it is closer to the allostasis model in Evans's course material (regulation that changes set points to meet demand; see Related) than to fixed-set-point homeostasis.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.22, where the functional indicators B and C wave above and below the rising fitness state A within each year (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--heterochronic-biochemical-adaptation-sequence]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--speed-barrier-is-genetic-ceiling-not-neural-block]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--homeostasis-vs-allostasis-mcewen-sterling]]\n=== framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md\n\n# Two Forms of Compensatory Adaptation: Distributed Loading Rises Gradually; Concentrated Loading Dips First, Then Rises Higher\n\n**What it is** — \"Two forms of compensatory adaptation, associated with enhancing the CAR by systematic training, which differ in volume and organisation of the training loads, have been identified and studied in explosive sports\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image):\n- **(A) Distributed loading:** \"a gradual increase in the functional indicators,\" with \"a moderate volume of continuous loading.\" \"periodic brief disturbances of the body's homeostasis occur and the current expenditure of energy resources is compensated during training.\" It \"follows traditional concepts for developing trainability and is typical for athletes of medium classification.\"\n- **(B) Concentrated loading:** \"a large, concentrated volume of special physical preparation means, concentrated in the first half of the training phase. These large volumes provoke a profound and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which is reflected by decrease of the functional indicators. Then, after the volume of loading is decreased, their increase accelerates and exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation. This way of organising loading in the preparatory mesocycles was devised for highly qualified athletes and has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n\nFig 6.23, \"Two forms of compensatory adaptation during the large preparation phase (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image and high-resolution crops of both panels), plots % change (100 = start) over 18 weeks, with dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12. Values read by eye, all \"about\":\n- **Panel A (distributed):** every curve rises from week 0. At week 18, starting strength about 144%, explosive strength about 128%; the three lower curves end at about 114%, 108% and 105%, matching the right-hand labels maximum strength, absolute strength and speed by end position. By that reading, speed peaks at about 113% near week 9 and then declines.\n- **Panel B (concentrated):** every curve falls first, to lows between about 83% and about 96% around weeks 4-6, crosses back above 100% around weeks 7-9, and ends at week 18 at about 157% (starting strength), 143% (explosive strength), 129% (maximum strength, after a peak of about 131% near week 16), 120% (speed) and 114% (absolute strength). The page does not let one tell which quality dips deepest.\n\nThe authors' further claim: \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion of the body's energy resources by concentrated loading during specific mesocycles of training (obviously within reasonable limits), the higher their subsequent super-restoration and the longer the new functional level is maintained\" (Verkhoshansky, 1977). \"The key issue, of course, is determining the optimal duration of concentrated loading, so as to avoid exhaustion and diminished performance\" (PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350).\n\nOn critics: Komarova (1984) \"correctly recognises that premature or prolonged application of concentrated loading can lead to overtraining, stagnation or injury,\" but \"errs in extrapolating this analysis to completely dismiss the periodic use of concentrated loading by qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the compensatory sequence: a large, prolonged homeostatic disturbance depresses function, and when volume drops the compensatory reaction overshoots. They cite Verkhoshansky (1977) and give no sample or data beyond Fig 6.23, which carries no study label.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: concentrated loading is the authors' choice for highly qualified athletes in preparatory mesocycles with time to absorb a dip of several weeks; distributed loading when performance must hold; at medium qualification the authors say both forms work (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the authors' split by qualification sits alongside that baseline split by strength level.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested across the library:** the authors say concentrated loading \"has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, noting fitness gains without reported effects on team practice or competition (p. 6), and placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality (p. 11) ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's \"concentrated in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page; its x-axis ends at 18 weeks, while the text's period is 18-22 weeks (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]). Inference: the \"deeper exhaustion, higher rebound\" claim sits on the same pages as the Bondarchuk result the authors read as \"calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350); the authors do not reconcile the two (see [[claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect]]). The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349); in 6.9.5 they say \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image), so it is not required there (see [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.23, where after concentrated loading starting strength ends at about 157% against about 144% with distributed loading over the same 18 weeks (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] (6.9.5)\n=== heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md\n\n# Block Durations for Enhancing the Adaptation Reserve: 5-6 Weeks Continuous or 3-4 Weeks Concentrated, Three Blocks With 7-10 Day Pauses, Then a Longer Recovery\n\n**What it is** — The authors give the durations \"vital for maximal enhancement of the body's CAR\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image):\n1. \"the appropriate duration of continuous training is 5-6 weeks, and with concentrated loading, the duration is 3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary for activating the compensatory processes.\"\n2. \"the trained athlete is able to tolerate three such sequential blocks of loading, separated by short recuperation pauses (7-10 days). After this, a longer recuperation period is necessary to facilitate stabilisation at the new level of adaptation.\"\n3. \"Thus, the time interval necessary for exhausting the body's CAR is between 18-22 weeks (see Fig 6.23). This period coincides with observations of highly qualified athletes training in different sports, including cyclic types (Zhikharevich, 1976; Sirenko, 1980).\"\n4. \"the body cannot indefinitely continue to adapt successively to training loads,\" and \"it is inappropriate to subject the body to extreme exhaustion of its energy reserves. This requires a longer recuperation period and has a negative effect on the subsequent stages of adaptation.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the CAR model: each block draws on a limited reserve, the recuperation pause lets the compensatory reaction occur, and after about three blocks the reserve is spent (see [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]]). No data for the durations are shown beyond the two citations for the 18-22 week period.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: sketching an off-season or preparatory phase for a trained athlete: up to three loading blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter pause, then a longer recovery before the next phase. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the 18-22 week ceiling is a reason to place a longer recovery period on that roadmap rather than run loading blocks back to back.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\", given as the duration that was \"determined\" for maximal enhancement of the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book gives, \"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, checked against the page image). The 4-8 weeks and \"up to 2 months\" agree with each other; the 3-4 weeks overlaps them only at 4 weeks, so the conflict is between the 3-4 week figure and the longer blocks. **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage*: \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370). 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts: \"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation\", and in principle its duration \"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). 1-3 weeks is the latency before the gains of a concentrated block appear (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The upper limits for one primary emphasis by motor quality are in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. Inference: one reading links block length to stage length (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis, inside the 4-12 week stage), but the book does not say so. Inference: 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (\"23-25% of the general volume for the year\", PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; \"23-25% of the general annual volume\", PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370), which makes the month the working unit; \"up to 2 months\" reads as a ceiling. **Unclear arithmetic:** three 5-6 week continuous blocks plus two 7-10 day pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week concentrated blocks plus two pauses come to about 11-15 weeks. The text does not say which form the 18-22 weeks refers to, and Fig 6.23, which it cites, spans only 18 weeks. Inference: the 18-22 week figure fits the continuous form better. The authors do not say how the pause length or the \"longer recuperation period\" should be set beyond these figures.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the rule, not a worked plan. Inference: a January-to-May preparatory phase of three 4-week concentrated blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter week, then a longer recovery period before summer training (the authors give no length for it).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]] (6.9.4), [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]] (Issurin: accumulation blocks up to about six weeks, with gains falling sharply after that), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]] (Bondarchuk's four-week blocks with two-week restorative blocks)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"dfcd8130-95fd-47cd-9ab5-4b37aabdd262","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:24.884Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md\n\n# Long-Term Speed-Strength Adaptation: Rises Every Year, Starts Each Year Below Last Year's Peak, Rises Less in the Highly Qualified, and Follows the Season's Peaks\n\n**What it is** — From \"observing the dynamics of an athlete's special physical preparedness indicators over many years of training,\" the authors state four conclusions, mainly for \"sports which require explosive effort\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n1. \"The absolute and mean level of special strength preparedness increases annually.\"\n2. \"The initial level of speed-strength in each annual cycle is lower than the level at the end of the preceding year's, but higher than at its beginning.\"\n3. \"The rise in speed-strength from year to year is larger for athletes of medium qualification than for highly qualified athletes.\"\n4. The annual trend \"is determined by the traditional periodisation of training for the given sport and competition calendar. One can clearly trace a twin peak in the dynamics of speed-strength with two competition stages, because the level of special strength preparedness is higher in the second competition stage than in the first. In types of sports with one competition season, speed-strength rises regularly and uniformly.\"\n\nFig 6.22, \"The dynamics of adaptation in sports training over several years (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a 400 dpi crop), is a schematic over Years 1-3 with no numeric axis (\"Relative levels\"): a thick rising line A labelled \"Fitness state\" whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3); two wavy lines B and C labelled \"Functional indicators\" that oscillate around A and dip near each year boundary; and a lower, slowly rising wavy line D labelled \"Skill.\" The text assigns B to bi-cyclic and C to mono-cyclic periodisation, and A to \"relatively stable, long-term adaptation over many years.\" D shows that \"adaptation in sport consists not only of increased motor potential, but also the improvement of the athlete's skills to more effectively utilise this potential\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors read the annual waves (B, C) as \"temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible\" compensatory changes riding on slower stable adaptation (A) (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image; see [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]]). No athletes, sports, sample sizes or citations are given for the four conclusions; \"Extensive research allows us to reach the following conclusions\" is the only basis stated, hence `supported` at most.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: set each year's expectations from these patterns: speed-strength at the start of a new year should sit below last year's best but above last year's start; a smaller annual gain in an advanced athlete is expected, not a failure. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: Fig 6.22's A and B/C lines draw that distinction, on a season scale rather than a daily one. Eric's baseline: in football \"you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: football's one long season is closest to the authors' single-season case, where speed-strength \"rises regularly and uniformly.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The conclusions come from explosive-strength sports; the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly, and that the problem lies \"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\": \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity (i.e. endurance), but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Inference: an endurance or conditioning test can show that work capacity changed without showing which adaptation produced the change. Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years. Inference: for an athlete whose speed-strength has plateaued across years, conclusion 1 no longer describes them; that is the limit the parabola refers to.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the \"twin peak\" in speed-strength with two competition stages, the second higher than the first (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]], [[principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors]], [[framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]\n=== claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md\n\n# Compensatory Adaptation Is Temporary and Comes First; Stable Adaptation Builds on It, and Has a Limit\n\n**What it is** — The authors split adaptation into two layers (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image; the figure it refers to is on PDF p. 358):\n- **Compensatory adaptation.** The annual changes in functional indicators (Fig 6.22, graphs B and C) \"are temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible.\" Their basis is \"compensatory mechanisms, i.e. reactions designed to maintain the necessary level of functioning for brief periods under demanding conditions which ultimately lead to stable adaptation.\" These \"gradually fade as adaptation takes place,\" and \"Insofar as these mechanisms precede adaptation, they should be designated pre-adaptation phenomena (Kuznyetsov, 1979).\"\n- **Long-term, stable adaptation** (graph A). \"compensatory adaptation is a prerequisite condition for the development of long-term, stable adaptation,\" which \"can progressively increase if the functional reconstruction during the course of compensatory adaptation is sufficient to produce a noticeable effect on the external interactions of the body and increase its special work capacity.\"\n\nThree further statements on the same page:\n1. \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process. Adaptation is the dynamic maintenance of a definite level of imbalance between the body and the environment\" (Bauer, 1935; Bernstein, 1966).\n2. The systems involved improve at different rates. This heterochronicity \"is determined by: the action of particular physiological systems in establishing long-term adaptation of a specific emphasis; differences in their reactiveness (or adaptive inertia); changes in the role of these systems at different stages of long-term adaptation.\"\n3. \"Long-term compensatory adaptation cannot continue indefinitely.\" Its effect \"diminishes with each repetition,\" described \"by a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time (Fig 6.22, graph A). This indicates that an athlete's general adaptation reserves have a limit that is genetically predetermined.\" The limit shows as \"waves in the dynamics of special work capacity (graphs B and C),\" caused partly by \"traditional periodisation\" but \"a more important reason is the exhaustion of the so-called current adaptation reserves (CAR) of the body.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' account is the one above: short-term compensations hold function under stress, and if they change performance enough, the body consolidates them. They state that the picture is clearest in explosive-strength sports, and that research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) \"has to be taken much further in its application to the development of sporting mastery, especially in endurance sports\"; until then they assume \"the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms, although at another level of the physiological systems\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348). Inference: the \"genetically predetermined\" limit is inferred from the shape of the curve, not from genetic data.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: treat a season's gains in functional indicators as provisional until they hold through a lower-load period; a quality that survives the transition phase has become stable adaptation in the authors' terms. When a block stops producing gains, the authors' model points to exhausted current reserves before it points to a genetic ceiling (see [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — No data are given for the parabola or the genetic limit. Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola, but no peak is drawn within the three years (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347). The \"imbalance\" definition is a theoretical position (Bauer, Bernstein), not a measured one. Inference: it is closer to the allostasis model in Evans's course material (regulation that changes set points to meet demand; see Related) than to fixed-set-point homeostasis.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.22, where the functional indicators B and C wave above and below the rising fitness state A within each year (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--heterochronic-biochemical-adaptation-sequence]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--speed-barrier-is-genetic-ceiling-not-neural-block]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--homeostasis-vs-allostasis-mcewen-sterling]]\n=== framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md\n\n# Two Forms of Compensatory Adaptation: Distributed Loading Rises Gradually; Concentrated Loading Dips First, Then Rises Higher\n\n**What it is** — \"Two forms of compensatory adaptation, associated with enhancing the CAR by systematic training, which differ in volume and organisation of the training loads, have been identified and studied in explosive sports\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image):\n- **(A) Distributed loading:** \"a gradual increase in the functional indicators,\" with \"a moderate volume of continuous loading.\" \"periodic brief disturbances of the body's homeostasis occur and the current expenditure of energy resources is compensated during training.\" It \"follows traditional concepts for developing trainability and is typical for athletes of medium classification.\"\n- **(B) Concentrated loading:** \"a large, concentrated volume of special physical preparation means, concentrated in the first half of the training phase. These large volumes provoke a profound and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which is reflected by decrease of the functional indicators. Then, after the volume of loading is decreased, their increase accelerates and exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation. This way of organising loading in the preparatory mesocycles was devised for highly qualified athletes and has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n\nFig 6.23, \"Two forms of compensatory adaptation during the large preparation phase (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image and high-resolution crops of both panels), plots % change (100 = start) over 18 weeks, with dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12. Values read by eye, all \"about\":\n- **Panel A (distributed):** every curve rises from week 0. At week 18, starting strength about 144%, explosive strength about 128%; the three lower curves end at about 114%, 108% and 105%, matching the right-hand labels maximum strength, absolute strength and speed by end position. By that reading, speed peaks at about 113% near week 9 and then declines.\n- **Panel B (concentrated):** every curve falls first, to lows between about 83% and about 96% around weeks 4-6, crosses back above 100% around weeks 7-9, and ends at week 18 at about 157% (starting strength), 143% (explosive strength), 129% (maximum strength, after a peak of about 131% near week 16), 120% (speed) and 114% (absolute strength). The page does not let one tell which quality dips deepest.\n\nThe authors' further claim: \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion of the body's energy resources by concentrated loading during specific mesocycles of training (obviously within reasonable limits), the higher their subsequent super-restoration and the longer the new functional level is maintained\" (Verkhoshansky, 1977). \"The key issue, of course, is determining the optimal duration of concentrated loading, so as to avoid exhaustion and diminished performance\" (PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350).\n\nOn critics: Komarova (1984) \"correctly recognises that premature or prolonged application of concentrated loading can lead to overtraining, stagnation or injury,\" but \"errs in extrapolating this analysis to completely dismiss the periodic use of concentrated loading by qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the compensatory sequence: a large, prolonged homeostatic disturbance depresses function, and when volume drops the compensatory reaction overshoots. They cite Verkhoshansky (1977) and give no sample or data beyond Fig 6.23, which carries no study label.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: concentrated loading is the authors' choice for highly qualified athletes in preparatory mesocycles with time to absorb a dip of several weeks; distributed loading when performance must hold; at medium qualification the authors say both forms work (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the authors' split by qualification sits alongside that baseline split by strength level.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested across the library:** the authors say concentrated loading \"has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, noting fitness gains without reported effects on team practice or competition (p. 6), and placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality (p. 11) ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's \"concentrated in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page; its x-axis ends at 18 weeks, while the text's period is 18-22 weeks (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]). Inference: the \"deeper exhaustion, higher rebound\" claim sits on the same pages as the Bondarchuk result the authors read as \"calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350); the authors do not reconcile the two (see [[claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect]]). The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349); in 6.9.5 they say \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image), so it is not required there (see [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.23, where after concentrated loading starting strength ends at about 157% against about 144% with distributed loading over the same 18 weeks (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] (6.9.5)\n=== heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md\n\n# Block Durations for Enhancing the Adaptation Reserve: 5-6 Weeks Continuous or 3-4 Weeks Concentrated, Three Blocks With 7-10 Day Pauses, Then a Longer Recovery\n\n**What it is** — The authors give the durations \"vital for maximal enhancement of the body's CAR\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image):\n1. \"the appropriate duration of continuous training is 5-6 weeks, and with concentrated loading, the duration is 3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary for activating the compensatory processes.\"\n2. \"the trained athlete is able to tolerate three such sequential blocks of loading, separated by short recuperation pauses (7-10 days). After this, a longer recuperation period is necessary to facilitate stabilisation at the new level of adaptation.\"\n3. \"Thus, the time interval necessary for exhausting the body's CAR is between 18-22 weeks (see Fig 6.23). This period coincides with observations of highly qualified athletes training in different sports, including cyclic types (Zhikharevich, 1976; Sirenko, 1980).\"\n4. \"the body cannot indefinitely continue to adapt successively to training loads,\" and \"it is inappropriate to subject the body to extreme exhaustion of its energy reserves. This requires a longer recuperation period and has a negative effect on the subsequent stages of adaptation.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the CAR model: each block draws on a limited reserve, the recuperation pause lets the compensatory reaction occur, and after about three blocks the reserve is spent (see [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]]). No data for the durations are shown beyond the two citations for the 18-22 week period.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: sketching an off-season or preparatory phase for a trained athlete: up to three loading blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter pause, then a longer recovery before the next phase. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the 18-22 week ceiling is a reason to place a longer recovery period on that roadmap rather than run loading blocks back to back.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\", given as the duration that was \"determined\" for maximal enhancement of the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book gives, \"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, checked against the page image). The 4-8 weeks and \"up to 2 months\" agree with each other; the 3-4 weeks overlaps them only at 4 weeks, so the conflict is between the 3-4 week figure and the longer blocks. **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage*: \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370). 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts: \"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation\", and in principle its duration \"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). 1-3 weeks is the latency before the gains of a concentrated block appear (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The upper limits for one primary emphasis by motor quality are in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. Inference: one reading links block length to stage length (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis, inside the 4-12 week stage), but the book does not say so. Inference: 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (\"23-25% of the general volume for the year\", PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; \"23-25% of the general annual volume\", PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370), which makes the month the working unit; \"up to 2 months\" reads as a ceiling. **Unclear arithmetic:** three 5-6 week continuous blocks plus two 7-10 day pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week concentrated blocks plus two pauses come to about 11-15 weeks. The text does not say which form the 18-22 weeks refers to, and Fig 6.23, which it cites, spans only 18 weeks. Inference: the 18-22 week figure fits the continuous form better. The authors do not say how the pause length or the \"longer recuperation period\" should be set beyond these figures.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the rule, not a worked plan. Inference: a January-to-May preparatory phase of three 4-week concentrated blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter week, then a longer recovery period before summer training (the authors give no length for it).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]] (6.9.4), [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]] (Issurin: accumulation blocks up to about six weeks, with gains falling sharply after that), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]] (Bondarchuk's four-week blocks with two-week restorative blocks)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5bee2596-4e6e-4ea9-85ed-f08142dc8cba","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
