{"parentUuid":"e708f8be-79ad-4a45-8392-a9c699700532","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpABfwfHD6Ddqosxuex","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TQKZfqhKhAeub58QTLFDAk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '13,$p' \"$f\"; done","description":"Print remaining three FIX entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9015,"cache_read_input_tokens":91185,"output_tokens":446,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":184},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9015},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":446,"cache_read_input_tokens":91185,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9015,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9015,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TQKZfqhKhAeub58QTLFDAk":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '13,$p' \"$f\"; done","description":"Print remaining three FIX entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpAAorXitvNTyHtozuL","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e38500cc-51db-4e0c-8eec-6d271e075701","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:29.037Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e38500cc-51db-4e0c-8eec-6d271e075701","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d3b98ac2-d94a-4ded-8d2a-6ab7c83cd263","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TQKZfqhKhAeub58QTLFDAk","type":"tool_result","content":"=== claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md\n\n# Bondarchuk (1982): 50% More Volume of the Same Programme Did Not Lengthen the Training Effect in Hammer Throwers\n\n**What it is** — The authors report: \"a study done by Bondarchuk (1982) which analysed the effect of different volumes of an identical training programme on a group of hammer-throwers. He found that a 50% increase in volume of loading had no significant effect on the duration of maintenance of the training effect and sporting performance. Although Bondarchuk stresses that this finding applies strictly to a particular group of athletes, it tends to confirm that major changes in intensity have a more profound effect on athletic state than comparable changes in volume. It also implies that calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity, thus corroborating the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity, as emphasized earlier in 6.2.2 (see also Table 6.1)\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image; the last phrase is italic in the original).\n\nTwo layers: Bondarchuk's finding (50% more volume, no significant change in how long the training effect and performance were maintained) and the authors' extrapolation (intensity matters more than volume).\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading. No sample size, volumes, durations or measures are given on the page.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: as one reason not to answer a stalled thrower or strength athlete with more of the same work. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume'\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the Bondarchuk result is consistent with that rule's premise that volume can drop without losing the effect, though it was not an in-season study.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — One group of hammer throwers, by Bondarchuk's own caveat \"strictly\" limited to them; hence `untested`. The study varied volume only, so \"intensity has a more profound effect\" is not shown by it. Inference: on the page before, the authors argue the opposite direction for volume: \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion of the body's energy resources by concentrated loading ... the higher their subsequent super-restoration and the longer the new functional level is maintained\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349), which makes concentrated volume the lever; the authors set the two side by side without reconciling them. The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-335 / printed pp. 323-324 (6.2.2 opens on PDF p. 334; Table 6.1 is on PDF p. 335).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Bondarchuk's hammer throwers, whose 50% volume increase did not change how long the training effect was maintained (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-strength-exercise-ceiling-effect]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]]\n=== principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md\n\n# Specialisation Sets a Primary Emphasis, Not an Exclusive System: Explosive Athletes Need Aerobic Efficiency, Endurance Athletes Need Strength\n\n**What it is** — \"Structural-functional specialisation refers to those stable, adaptive changes that are produced by the specific form of conditioning imposed by a given sport,\" and \"those systems which are of major importance for success in sport undergo an accelerated rate of functional growth\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image). The specifics of adaptation are set by \"the work regime in sports, and the gradual, but steady increase in complexity of the body's external interactions associated with the growth of sporting proficiency\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image).\n\nUsing the sport groups, \"specialisation by physiological system means that acyclic sports rely predominantly on anaerobic processes and functional hypertrophy of the motor apparatus, whereas cyclic sports place major reliance on aerobic processes. In complex (combined) sports, there is an intense functional development of both the motor apparatus and the energy production systems\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The page says \"the classification scheme of 6.5\"; the scheme is in 6.7.\n\nThe new point is the qualifier: \"specialisation in the first two cases does not imply that development is either strictly aerobic or anaerobic, but rather that there is a particular primary emphasis of functional specialisation. One can successfully develop a high level of explosive strength in acyclic locomotion only when one possesses sufficiently high aerobic efficiency. At the same time, improving the efficiency of the energy producing systems in cyclic locomotion should be based on highly developed muscular strength and local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). For cyclic sports: \"In cyclic sports, strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role. Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength, together with a rise in the athlete's qualification, the qualities of dynamic strength-endurance and power accelerate the increase in functional strength (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for why explosive strength needs aerobic efficiency on this page. For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only. Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point. On cost they add: \"energy expenditure being almost proportional to the fourth power of running speed (Sargent, 1926),\" with the rising cost met by \"a greater reliance being placed on anaerobic processes\" (Henry, 1951; Margaria, 1963) (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The fourth-power figure carries only that 1926 citation, and no later check is given.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when a speed-power programme drops all aerobic work, or an endurance programme drops strength work, this principle argues for keeping a supporting dose of the other. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 123, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that is one plausible route for the authors' \"sufficiently high aerobic efficiency,\" though the authors do not give it. Eric's baseline: football's priority stack names \"alactic and aerobic capacity/power\" as Goals 1 and 2 (line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1). Inference: the baseline already pairs the explosive and aerobic systems, as the authors' qualifier does.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Sufficiently high\" is not quantified, and no study is cited for the acyclic half of the claim. Inference: it does not license large aerobic volumes for power athletes; the claim is a minimum, not a target. The heterochronicity point on the same page (\"the body adapts to external influences only when they become necessary for the further growth of sporting proficiency\") is already covered by the Chapter 2 heterochronicity entry in Related and is not re-atomized here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: improving the triple jump \"requires developing the ability of the motor apparatus to produce great power,\" and a longer jump, gained mainly through running speed, brings \"a significant increase in complexity of the interaction of the feet with the support at each take-off, a situation that requires significant improvement in reactive ability\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]] (5.4.3, specialisation by system and by ability), [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov]]\n=== claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md\n\n# Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\n\n**What it is** — The authors report Vankov (1978) on multi-year emphasis (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image): \"In the multi-year training of sprinters, up to Class I-CMS (Class I - Candidate for Master of Sport), the chief emphasis is on developing general (aerobic) endurance and speed, whereas at MS-MSIC (Master of Sport - Master of Sport, International Class) level the emphasis is on developing speed (anaerobic) endurance. In the training of distance athletes of all qualification levels, the chief emphasis is on developing aerobic productivity, while at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity (Vankov, 1978).\"\n\nThe surrounding point: kinesiological patterns across sports \"are approximately equivalent, although there are some qualitative and quantitative differences\"; \"sprint swimmers have higher rates and developmental levels of anaerobic productivity than distance swimmers, whereas aerobic productivity is superior for distance swimmers. This is determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors attribute the swimmer differences to training emphasis; no mechanism is given for the qualification shift. Inference: it fits the convergence principle, in which training effects move toward the sport's specific regime as mastery rises (see Related).\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: in long-term plans for sprinters, a broad aerobic and speed base below national-class level, with speed endurance becoming the main emphasis only at the top. Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Vankov's sprinter sequence also starts from general capacity and narrows toward the event's specific demand.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear as printed.** The distance-athlete sentence (\"distance athletes\", not runners) says the chief emphasis is aerobic \"of all qualification levels\" and then that \"at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity,\" which contradicts \"all.\" Inference: the likely meaning is that aerobic work stays the chief emphasis at every level and anaerobic work is added at the top, but the page does not say so. Soviet classification levels (Class I, CMS, MS, MSIC) do not map directly onto college levels, and no data, sample or event distances are given; hence `supported` at most.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: sprint swimmers with higher anaerobic and distance swimmers with higher aerobic productivity, \"determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]], [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table]], [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--endurance-event-transfer-range-widens-with-distance]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"18d8f6ec-ec36-4ef1-a6f7-124aea56fe09","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:56:29.260Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md\n\n# Bondarchuk (1982): 50% More Volume of the Same Programme Did Not Lengthen the Training Effect in Hammer Throwers\n\n**What it is** — The authors report: \"a study done by Bondarchuk (1982) which analysed the effect of different volumes of an identical training programme on a group of hammer-throwers. He found that a 50% increase in volume of loading had no significant effect on the duration of maintenance of the training effect and sporting performance. Although Bondarchuk stresses that this finding applies strictly to a particular group of athletes, it tends to confirm that major changes in intensity have a more profound effect on athletic state than comparable changes in volume. It also implies that calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity, thus corroborating the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity, as emphasized earlier in 6.2.2 (see also Table 6.1)\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image; the last phrase is italic in the original).\n\nTwo layers: Bondarchuk's finding (50% more volume, no significant change in how long the training effect and performance were maintained) and the authors' extrapolation (intensity matters more than volume).\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading. No sample size, volumes, durations or measures are given on the page.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: as one reason not to answer a stalled thrower or strength athlete with more of the same work. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume'\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the Bondarchuk result is consistent with that rule's premise that volume can drop without losing the effect, though it was not an in-season study.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — One group of hammer throwers, by Bondarchuk's own caveat \"strictly\" limited to them; hence `untested`. The study varied volume only, so \"intensity has a more profound effect\" is not shown by it. Inference: on the page before, the authors argue the opposite direction for volume: \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion of the body's energy resources by concentrated loading ... the higher their subsequent super-restoration and the longer the new functional level is maintained\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349), which makes concentrated volume the lever; the authors set the two side by side without reconciling them. The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-335 / printed pp. 323-324 (6.2.2 opens on PDF p. 334; Table 6.1 is on PDF p. 335).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Bondarchuk's hammer throwers, whose 50% volume increase did not change how long the training effect was maintained (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-strength-exercise-ceiling-effect]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]]\n=== principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md\n\n# Specialisation Sets a Primary Emphasis, Not an Exclusive System: Explosive Athletes Need Aerobic Efficiency, Endurance Athletes Need Strength\n\n**What it is** — \"Structural-functional specialisation refers to those stable, adaptive changes that are produced by the specific form of conditioning imposed by a given sport,\" and \"those systems which are of major importance for success in sport undergo an accelerated rate of functional growth\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image). The specifics of adaptation are set by \"the work regime in sports, and the gradual, but steady increase in complexity of the body's external interactions associated with the growth of sporting proficiency\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image).\n\nUsing the sport groups, \"specialisation by physiological system means that acyclic sports rely predominantly on anaerobic processes and functional hypertrophy of the motor apparatus, whereas cyclic sports place major reliance on aerobic processes. In complex (combined) sports, there is an intense functional development of both the motor apparatus and the energy production systems\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The page says \"the classification scheme of 6.5\"; the scheme is in 6.7.\n\nThe new point is the qualifier: \"specialisation in the first two cases does not imply that development is either strictly aerobic or anaerobic, but rather that there is a particular primary emphasis of functional specialisation. One can successfully develop a high level of explosive strength in acyclic locomotion only when one possesses sufficiently high aerobic efficiency. At the same time, improving the efficiency of the energy producing systems in cyclic locomotion should be based on highly developed muscular strength and local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). For cyclic sports: \"In cyclic sports, strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role. Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength, together with a rise in the athlete's qualification, the qualities of dynamic strength-endurance and power accelerate the increase in functional strength (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for why explosive strength needs aerobic efficiency on this page. For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only. Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point. On cost they add: \"energy expenditure being almost proportional to the fourth power of running speed (Sargent, 1926),\" with the rising cost met by \"a greater reliance being placed on anaerobic processes\" (Henry, 1951; Margaria, 1963) (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The fourth-power figure carries only that 1926 citation, and no later check is given.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when a speed-power programme drops all aerobic work, or an endurance programme drops strength work, this principle argues for keeping a supporting dose of the other. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 123, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that is one plausible route for the authors' \"sufficiently high aerobic efficiency,\" though the authors do not give it. Eric's baseline: football's priority stack names \"alactic and aerobic capacity/power\" as Goals 1 and 2 (line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1). Inference: the baseline already pairs the explosive and aerobic systems, as the authors' qualifier does.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Sufficiently high\" is not quantified, and no study is cited for the acyclic half of the claim. Inference: it does not license large aerobic volumes for power athletes; the claim is a minimum, not a target. The heterochronicity point on the same page (\"the body adapts to external influences only when they become necessary for the further growth of sporting proficiency\") is already covered by the Chapter 2 heterochronicity entry in Related and is not re-atomized here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: improving the triple jump \"requires developing the ability of the motor apparatus to produce great power,\" and a longer jump, gained mainly through running speed, brings \"a significant increase in complexity of the interaction of the feet with the support at each take-off, a situation that requires significant improvement in reactive ability\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]] (5.4.3, specialisation by system and by ability), [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov]]\n=== claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md\n\n# Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\n\n**What it is** — The authors report Vankov (1978) on multi-year emphasis (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image): \"In the multi-year training of sprinters, up to Class I-CMS (Class I - Candidate for Master of Sport), the chief emphasis is on developing general (aerobic) endurance and speed, whereas at MS-MSIC (Master of Sport - Master of Sport, International Class) level the emphasis is on developing speed (anaerobic) endurance. In the training of distance athletes of all qualification levels, the chief emphasis is on developing aerobic productivity, while at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity (Vankov, 1978).\"\n\nThe surrounding point: kinesiological patterns across sports \"are approximately equivalent, although there are some qualitative and quantitative differences\"; \"sprint swimmers have higher rates and developmental levels of anaerobic productivity than distance swimmers, whereas aerobic productivity is superior for distance swimmers. This is determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors attribute the swimmer differences to training emphasis; no mechanism is given for the qualification shift. Inference: it fits the convergence principle, in which training effects move toward the sport's specific regime as mastery rises (see Related).\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: in long-term plans for sprinters, a broad aerobic and speed base below national-class level, with speed endurance becoming the main emphasis only at the top. Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Vankov's sprinter sequence also starts from general capacity and narrows toward the event's specific demand.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear as printed.** The distance-athlete sentence (\"distance athletes\", not runners) says the chief emphasis is aerobic \"of all qualification levels\" and then that \"at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity,\" which contradicts \"all.\" Inference: the likely meaning is that aerobic work stays the chief emphasis at every level and anaerobic work is added at the top, but the page does not say so. Soviet classification levels (Class I, CMS, MS, MSIC) do not map directly onto college levels, and no data, sample or event distances are given; hence `supported` at most.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: sprint swimmers with higher anaerobic and distance swimmers with higher aerobic productivity, \"determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]], [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table]], [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--endurance-event-transfer-range-widens-with-distance]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e38500cc-51db-4e0c-8eec-6d271e075701","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
