{"parentUuid":"a968931d-e77f-49c3-b1c0-ccb20f2e6449","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpFTFSdbhgcCZPBDQzA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YDPvMdr8EjSJ3EHFJtBSHZ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"f=$(find /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge -name \"issurin-2016-benefits*\"); echo \"$f\"; wc -l \"$f\"; grep -n -iE \"verkhoshansky|concentrated|insufficient|combat|team|swim|narrow|p\\. ?6|p\\. ?11|\\(6\\)|\\(11\\)|A-T-R|ATR\" \"$f\" | head -40","description":"Locate Issurin 2016 brief and grep key claims"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2404,"cache_read_input_tokens":124522,"output_tokens":352,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":90},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2404},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":352,"cache_read_input_tokens":124522,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2404,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2404,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01YDPvMdr8EjSJ3EHFJtBSHZ":{"command":"f=$(find /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge -name \"issurin-2016-benefits*\"); echo \"$f\"; wc -l \"$f\"; grep -n -iE \"verkhoshansky|concentrated|insufficient|combat|team|swim|narrow|p\\. ?6|p\\. ?11|\\(6\\)|\\(11\\)|A-T-R|ATR\" \"$f\" | head -40","description":"Locate Issurin 2016 brief and grep key claims"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffpFLX8sVftSDivcEVt1","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"79c443a8-3344-4107-9747-009c21613be8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:57:40.051Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"79c443a8-3344-4107-9747-009c21613be8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9f22f8c8-00fe-4792-8e01-141619e30d3b","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YDPvMdr8EjSJ3EHFJtBSHZ","type":"tool_result","content":"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md\n      77 /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md\n26:This review distinguishes concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block models and provides a useful conceptual vocabulary for discussing concentrated workloads and sequencing.\n34:- The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings. [p. 11]\n40:- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\n46:- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\n47:- In an elite-swimmer example, specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes. [p. 6]\n48:- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\n49:- The review reports support from 28 studies for multi-targeted block programs, but the designs, populations, outcomes, and comparator quality varied. [p. 11]\n50:- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\n51:- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\n56:- Fitness improvements are not interchangeable with sport-specific performance, availability, or competitive outcomes. [p. 6]\n59:- The strong conclusion favoring multi-targeted block periodization is the author's synthesis and should not be treated as settled comparative evidence. [p. 11]\n70:| Concentrated-unidirectional evidence and performance gaps | p. 6 | narrative synthesis |\n71:| Multi-targeted evidence claim and applicability boundaries | p. 11 | narrative synthesis/limitation |\n77:- [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]: Supertraining 5.3.3's system of successive concentrated blocks; it cites this review's limits for team, combat and swimming preparation as the case against using that system there","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c5f1b143-96a6-4077-b8dc-0499ec61eae7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:57:40.295Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md\n      77 /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md\n26:This review distinguishes concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block models and provides a useful conceptual vocabulary for discussing concentrated workloads and sequencing.\n34:- The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings. [p. 11]\n40:- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\n46:- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\n47:- In an elite-swimmer example, specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes. [p. 6]\n48:- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\n49:- The review reports support from 28 studies for multi-targeted block programs, but the designs, populations, outcomes, and comparator quality varied. [p. 11]\n50:- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\n51:- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\n56:- Fitness improvements are not interchangeable with sport-specific performance, availability, or competitive outcomes. [p. 6]\n59:- The strong conclusion favoring multi-targeted block periodization is the author's synthesis and should not be treated as settled comparative evidence. [p. 11]\n70:| Concentrated-unidirectional evidence and performance gaps | p. 6 | narrative synthesis |\n71:| Multi-targeted evidence claim and applicability boundaries | p. 11 | narrative synthesis/limitation |\n77:- [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]: Supertraining 5.3.3's system of successive concentrated blocks; it cites this review's limits for team, combat and swimming preparation as the case against using that system there","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"79c443a8-3344-4107-9747-009c21613be8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
