{"parentUuid":"61ca9405-7cb6-4897-a769-9a2cd8faeec5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TjN1tsF5VioLrWKeaz953L","type":"tool_result","content":"=== principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md\n\n# Load Must Sit Inside the Current Adaptation Reserve Window: Too Little Wastes It, Too Much Exhausts It\n\n**What it is** — The authors make the current adaptation reserves (CAR) a programming rule (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image): \"At any instant, the body has a definite reserve potential, i.e. a capability of responding to adapt to external influences and raise itself to a new functional level,\" limited by \"definite physiological mechanisms.\" Hence \"the intensity, volume and duration of the training influences determine the optimal development of the body's CAR. If the magnitude of these are below certain thresholds, the body will not fully utilise its CAR, whereas, if they exceed it, exhaustion of reserve potential will occur and the training effect will be low or negative.\"\n\nThe goal follows: \"one can consider training organisation to be effective when it produces the maximum growth of the body's CAR by means of the appropriate volume of loading.\" Useful programming therefore needs \"means of assessing the body's CAR in each case, together with criteria for determining the contents and volume of the training loads,\" and a quantitative approach \"is based, in particular, on assessments of a number of bio-chemical indicators of energy exchange processes (Beckman et al, 1961; Yakovlev, 1977)\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image).\n\nThe authors place this inside the older three-phase schemes of form: \"(a) the growth of trainability, (b) sporting form, (c) loss of trainability (Letunov, 1952)\"; \"(a) adaptation, (b) the highest sport work-capacity, (c) re-adaptation (Prokor, 1959),\" where re-adaptation is \"decrease in work capacity as the limits of adaptability are exceeded\" (PDF pp. 359-360 / printed pp. 348-349); and Matveyev's \"acquisition, preservation and brief loss of sporting form (Matveyev, 1964)\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). \"Prokor\" is the book's spelling.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is a threshold model: a reserve with a fixed size at any moment is under-used by small loads and depleted by large ones. They give no measured threshold. Inference: Bompa's threshold-of-adaptation entry (Related) sets out three outcomes of loading (an increasing, varied load, adaptation; an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation) at the level of single loads; the authors apply it to a whole training phase.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when a block produces no gain, ask first whether the load was below threshold (too little) or beyond it (exhaustion); the remedy is opposite in the two cases. The authors' wave signal (see Related) is one way to tell: waves in special work capacity mark the reserve running out. Eric's baseline: the mRSI decision read uses individualized thresholds, \"Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to diagnose signal versus noise\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 75, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)). Inference: that is a practical, non-biochemical stand-in for the \"means of assessing the body's CAR\" the authors ask for, read at the weekly scale.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The page names biochemical indicators only in general and cites 1961 and 1977 work; no assay, threshold or validated CAR measure is given. Chapter 1 gives two conflicting definitions of CAR (the readily available energy versus the deep adaptation energy; see [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]]); this section does not settle which is meant. Inference: treat CAR as a planning concept, not a measurable quantity.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own application follows on the same page: the durations of continuous and concentrated loading blocks that they say maximally enhance the CAR (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]], [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold]], [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]] (6.9.4, volume as the \"disturbance of the body's homeostasis\")\n=== heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance.md\n\n# After Heavy Disturbance, Cut Total Volume and Add General Physical Preparation; the Deeper the Disturbance, the Longer the Restorative Stage\n\n**What it is** — The authors' rule for restorative stages after concentrated loading (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image): \"Training organisation which elicits extensive and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis necessitates the inclusion of suitable restorative stages for triggering a compensatory reaction, thereby eliminating inappropriately phased peaking of the various functional indicators and stabilising the body at the new functional level. The general volume of the load should be decreased at this time and General Physical Preparation means should be introduced into training so as to intensify the compensatory reaction. The more extensive and prolonged the disturbance of homeostasis, the longer the restorative stage should be.\"\n\nThree parts:\n1. **Purpose:** trigger the compensatory reaction, prevent the functional indicators from peaking out of phase with each other, and stabilise the new level.\n2. **Content:** lower total volume, and bring in General Physical Preparation (GPP) means.\n3. **Length:** scaled to the size and duration of the preceding disturbance.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the compensatory model: the gains of a concentrated block appear only after volume falls (\"after the volume of loading is decreased, their increase accelerates\"; PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). They do not say why GPP intensifies the reaction, and give no data for it.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: plan the stage after any deliberately heavy block as a change of content, not only a cut in load: lower volume of special means, more general work. Inference: size its length to the block before it, longer after a longer or heavier block. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery capacity must be 'earned' by training hard enough first\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 151, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' rule is the other half of that statement: once the hard block has been done, the restorative stage is part of the plan.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — No durations are given for the restorative stage here. In 5.3.3 the book gives a 1-3 week latency to gains after 4-8 week concentrated blocks (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]), and on the page before this one a 7-10 day pause between blocks and a longer, unspecified recuperation after three blocks (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). Bompa & Haff state a similar scaling rule (\"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve\"), as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]; that is agreement on direction, not on a number. Inference: \"GPP\" here is not defined; the means chosen should still not undo the quality just built.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: after a three-week concentrated jump and strength block, a sprinter's next week drops the depth jumps and heavy squats, keeps short sprints, and adds general circuits and aerobic work.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n=== heuristic--expectancy-motivation-break-distant-goals-into-attainable-steps.md\n\n# Expectancy Motivation: Effort Follows Attractiveness Times Believed Attainability, So Break Distant Goals Into Reachable Steps\n\n**What it is** — The model is Vroom's Expectancy Theory of Motivation, which the authors take from Tellier (1978) and apply to coaching athletes (PDF p. 353 / printed p. 342, checked against the page image). \"it is not merely a specific goal, but the individual perception of that goal which motivates people.\" Motivational Force depends on:\n- \"the perceived attractiveness (valence V) of the goal or outcome\"\n- \"the perceived probability of achieving that goal or outcome (one's personal belief, B, that success is likely)\"\n\ncombined as \"their arithmetic product F = V X B.\" Scales: attractiveness \"from -5 (extremely undesirable) to +5 (extremely desirable),\" belief \"from 0 (total disbelief) to 1 (total belief)\"; the overall force \"is the sum of the motivational forces calculated from the products ... for all the important goals or outcomes associated with carrying out the task.\"\n\nThe authors' applications: if a goal is \"extremely attractive, but you believe it to be far outside your reach, you will experience a small Motivational Force,\" so \"it is desirable to break down a large or distant goal into smaller goals to be achieved over a period or at a rate which you perceive to be more matched to your capabilities or experience.\" And: \"a manager or leader can motivate athletes strongly if he appreciates that motivation depends on perceptions of goal attractiveness and attainability. He can improve performance either by modifying the task accordingly or by altering the athlete's perceptions of the task and its outcomes\" (PDF p. 353 / printed p. 342).\n\n**Why it works** — The mechanism is the multiplication itself: if belief is near zero, attractiveness cannot compensate. The authors give no study and say \"Detailed examples of this theory are outside the scope of this book\" (PDF p. 353 / printed p. 342).\n\n**When to apply** — Setting targets with an athlete. Inference: check both terms. A target the athlete values but does not believe in (a large testing jump, a return-to-play date) gets split into stages the athlete can see as reachable. A target the athlete can reach but does not value needs its outcome made worth something to that athlete. Eric's baseline: \"New movement patterns are expected to take roughly 3-4 weeks of practice to become efficient\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 171, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that expected timeline is one way to keep belief realistic when a new exercise is introduced, so early clumsiness is not read as failure.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors present the model without data, and the scales (-5 to +5, 0 to 1) are Vroom's as reported, not measured here. The page gives F as a single product and then as a sum over outcomes; both appear, for one goal and for a task with several outcomes. Inference: \"altering the athlete's perceptions\" is not licence to inflate belief beyond the athlete's real capability, since a target that is then missed lowers belief for the next one.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own, in general form: a goal that is highly attractive but believed far out of reach yields little effort until it is broken into smaller goals matched to capability (PDF p. 353 / printed p. 342). Inference: an athlete told to add 100 kg to a squat over a year is given four quarterly targets instead.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity]], [[framework--situational-leadership-coach-matches-style-to-athlete-maturity]], [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]], [[research-papers/halson-2017-amazing-athletes-with-ordinary-habits-why-is-changing-behavior-so-difficult]] (recommends athlete-chosen measurable goals and small progressive changes; agreement)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"0f429420-2c19-49cf-851b-099540393da9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:15.677Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md\n\n# Load Must Sit Inside the Current Adaptation Reserve Window: Too Little Wastes It, Too Much Exhausts It\n\n**What it is** — The authors make the current adaptation reserves (CAR) a programming rule (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image): \"At any instant, the body has a definite reserve potential, i.e. a capability of responding to adapt to external influences and raise itself to a new functional level,\" limited by \"definite physiological mechanisms.\" Hence \"the intensity, volume and duration of the training influences determine the optimal development of the body's CAR. If the magnitude of these are below certain thresholds, the body will not fully utilise its CAR, whereas, if they exceed it, exhaustion of reserve potential will occur and the training effect will be low or negative.\"\n\nThe goal follows: \"one can consider training organisation to be effective when it produces the maximum growth of the body's CAR by means of the appropriate volume of loading.\" Useful programming therefore needs \"means of assessing the body's CAR in each case, together with criteria for determining the contents and volume of the training loads,\" and a quantitative approach \"is based, in particular, on assessments of a number of bio-chemical indicators of energy exchange processes (Beckman et al, 1961; Yakovlev, 1977)\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image).\n\nThe authors place this inside the older three-phase schemes of form: \"(a) the growth of trainability, (b) sporting form, (c) loss of trainability (Letunov, 1952)\"; \"(a) adaptation, (b) the highest sport work-capacity, (c) re-adaptation (Prokor, 1959),\" where re-adaptation is \"decrease in work capacity as the limits of adaptability are exceeded\" (PDF pp. 359-360 / printed pp. 348-349); and Matveyev's \"acquisition, preservation and brief loss of sporting form (Matveyev, 1964)\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). \"Prokor\" is the book's spelling.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is a threshold model: a reserve with a fixed size at any moment is under-used by small loads and depleted by large ones. They give no measured threshold. Inference: Bompa's threshold-of-adaptation entry (Related) sets out three outcomes of loading (an increasing, varied load, adaptation; an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation) at the level of single loads; the authors apply it to a whole training phase.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when a block produces no gain, ask first whether the load was below threshold (too little) or beyond it (exhaustion); the remedy is opposite in the two cases. The authors' wave signal (see Related) is one way to tell: waves in special work capacity mark the reserve running out. Eric's baseline: the mRSI decision read uses individualized thresholds, \"Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to diagnose signal versus noise\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 75, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)). Inference: that is a practical, non-biochemical stand-in for the \"means of assessing the body's CAR\" the authors ask for, read at the weekly scale.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The page names biochemical indicators only in general and cites 1961 and 1977 work; no assay, threshold or validated CAR measure is given. Chapter 1 gives two conflicting definitions of CAR (the readily available energy versus the deep adaptation energy; see [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]]); this section does not settle which is meant. Inference: treat CAR as a planning concept, not a measurable quantity.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own application follows on the same page: the durations of continuous and concentrated loading blocks that they say maximally enhance the CAR (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]], [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold]], [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]] (6.9.4, volume as the \"disturbance of the body's homeostasis\")\n=== heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance.md\n\n# After Heavy Disturbance, Cut Total Volume and Add General Physical Preparation; the Deeper the Disturbance, the Longer the Restorative Stage\n\n**What it is** — The authors' rule for restorative stages after concentrated loading (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image): \"Training organisation which elicits extensive and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis necessitates the inclusion of suitable restorative stages for triggering a compensatory reaction, thereby eliminating inappropriately phased peaking of the various functional indicators and stabilising the body at the new functional level. The general volume of the load should be decreased at this time and General Physical Preparation means should be introduced into training so as to intensify the compensatory reaction. The more extensive and prolonged the disturbance of homeostasis, the longer the restorative stage should be.\"\n\nThree parts:\n1. **Purpose:** trigger the compensatory reaction, prevent the functional indicators from peaking out of phase with each other, and stabilise the new level.\n2. **Content:** lower total volume, and bring in General Physical Preparation (GPP) means.\n3. **Length:** scaled to the size and duration of the preceding disturbance.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the compensatory model: the gains of a concentrated block appear only after volume falls (\"after the volume of loading is decreased, their increase accelerates\"; PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). They do not say why GPP intensifies the reaction, and give no data for it.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: plan the stage after any deliberately heavy block as a change of content, not only a cut in load: lower volume of special means, more general work. Inference: size its length to the block before it, longer after a longer or heavier block. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery capacity must be 'earned' by training hard enough first\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 151, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' rule is the other half of that statement: once the hard block has been done, the restorative stage is part of the plan.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — No durations are given for the restorative stage here. In 5.3.3 the book gives a 1-3 week latency to gains after 4-8 week concentrated blocks (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]), and on the page before this one a 7-10 day pause between blocks and a longer, unspecified recuperation after three blocks (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). Bompa & Haff state a similar scaling rule (\"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve\"), as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]; that is agreement on direction, not on a number. Inference: \"GPP\" here is not defined; the means chosen should still not undo the quality just built.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: after a three-week concentrated jump and strength block, a sprinter's next week drops the depth jumps and heavy squats, keeps short sprints, and adds general circuits and aerobic work.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n=== heuristic--expectancy-motivation-break-distant-goals-into-attainable-steps.md\n\n# Expectancy Motivation: Effort Follows Attractiveness Times Believed Attainability, So Break Distant Goals Into Reachable Steps\n\n**What it is** — The model is Vroom's Expectancy Theory of Motivation, which the authors take from Tellier (1978) and apply to coaching athletes (PDF p. 353 / printed p. 342, checked against the page image). \"it is not merely a specific goal, but the individual perception of that goal which motivates people.\" Motivational Force depends on:\n- \"the perceived attractiveness (valence V) of the goal or outcome\"\n- \"the perceived probability of achieving that goal or outcome (one's personal belief, B, that success is likely)\"\n\ncombined as \"their arithmetic product F = V X B.\" Scales: attractiveness \"from -5 (extremely undesirable) to +5 (extremely desirable),\" belief \"from 0 (total disbelief) to 1 (total belief)\"; the overall force \"is the sum of the motivational forces calculated from the products ... for all the important goals or outcomes associated with carrying out the task.\"\n\nThe authors' applications: if a goal is \"extremely attractive, but you believe it to be far outside your reach, you will experience a small Motivational Force,\" so \"it is desirable to break down a large or distant goal into smaller goals to be achieved over a period or at a rate which you perceive to be more matched to your capabilities or experience.\" And: \"a manager or leader can motivate athletes strongly if he appreciates that motivation depends on perceptions of goal attractiveness and attainability. He can improve performance either by modifying the task accordingly or by altering the athlete's perceptions of the task and its outcomes\" (PDF p. 353 / printed p. 342).\n\n**Why it works** — The mechanism is the multiplication itself: if belief is near zero, attractiveness cannot compensate. The authors give no study and say \"Detailed examples of this theory are outside the scope of this book\" (PDF p. 353 / printed p. 342).\n\n**When to apply** — Setting targets with an athlete. Inference: check both terms. A target the athlete values but does not believe in (a large testing jump, a return-to-play date) gets split into stages the athlete can see as reachable. A target the athlete can reach but does not value needs its outcome made worth something to that athlete. Eric's baseline: \"New movement patterns are expected to take roughly 3-4 weeks of practice to become efficient\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 171, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that expected timeline is one way to keep belief realistic when a new exercise is introduced, so early clumsiness is not read as failure.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors present the model without data, and the scales (-5 to +5, 0 to 1) are Vroom's as reported, not measured here. The page gives F as a single product and then as a sum over outcomes; both appear, for one goal and for a task with several outcomes. Inference: \"altering the athlete's perceptions\" is not licence to inflate belief beyond the athlete's real capability, since a target that is then missed lowers belief for the next one.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own, in general form: a goal that is highly attractive but believed far out of reach yields little effort until it is broken into smaller goals matched to capability (PDF p. 353 / printed p. 342). Inference: an athlete told to add 100 kg to a squat over a year is given four quarterly targets instead.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity]], [[framework--situational-leadership-coach-matches-style-to-athlete-maturity]], [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]], [[research-papers/halson-2017-amazing-athletes-with-ordinary-habits-why-is-changing-behavior-so-difficult]] (recommends athlete-chosen measurable goals and small progressive changes; agreement)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"61ca9405-7cb6-4897-a769-9a2cd8faeec5","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
