05 106 115 116 117 123 151 153 171 183 185 204; do echo \"$n: $(sed -n \"${n}p\" $B | cut -c1-260)\"; done; echo; ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 -c \"\nimport json\nT=json.load(open('staging/_tmp/r2_6b_text.json'))\nt=' '.join(T[p] for p in ['359','360']).replace('\\n',' ')\nfor s in ['Letunov','Prokor','Matveyev, 1964','Beckman','Yakovlev, 1977','re-adaptation','highest sport']: print(s, s in t)\nt=T['353'].replace('\\n',' ')\nfor s in ['Tellier','-5','+5','arithmetic product','outside the scope','sum of the motivational']: print(s, s in t)\n\"","description":"Print cited baseline lines and check names in source"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffpJLoqAVfTtxnRrqfJN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e5f24520-e00b-475c-b250-5a6357903539","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:23.214Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e5f24520-e00b-475c-b250-5a6357903539","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvXzco7NonQar2gouneYBW","type":"tool_result","content":"27: - Applied Performance's stated mission is to \"drive elite performance ... by leveraging science and technology through education and relationships,\" enabling the high-performance team to answer questions, ask better questions, and discover new ones — not to ha\n30: - Data collection is judged on cost versus insight: \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention. When the insight stops justifying that cost, it's time to stop collecting.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n32: - General rule for any test or technology, stated as a standing rule rather than a fresh call each time. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q3): \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-0\n59: - Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.7] (See the cadence bullet below: ther\n64: - Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation) — both are tracked, but they answer different questions. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-clas\n75: - The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds, not a single cut-off or position-group thresholds. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2): \"Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized.\" Individualized against: Eric (intervie\n101: - The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 202\n105: - Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build stren\n106: - Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression, specifically to avoid staleness whil\n115: - A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands (e.g., for football: prepared for fall camp first, then the first game). **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31]\n116: - For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentall\n117: - Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**\n123: - For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity — a better-developed aerobic system means more available energy on play 10, 11, or 12 of a drive, not longer continuous\n151: - Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first; recovery technology (Theragun, Normatec, cryo) is treated as useful but not a substitute for managing training stress and dose correctly. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-tran\n153: - Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q23): \"It's handled through training-load design alone. We're not tracking anything objective.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28\n171: - New movement patterns are expected to take roughly 3-4 weeks of practice to become efficient and low-energy-cost, which informs how much proficiency to expect when a new exercise or drill is introduced. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-tra\n183: - An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reachi\n185: - In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in football you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November as you get closer to the\n204: - Curriculum design principle: interns need both the \"why\" (conceptual education) and the \"how\" (hands-on technical training) each week, and should leave the program with a professional portfolio of real work products (dashboards, reports, presentations, infog\n\nLetunov True\nProkor True\nMatveyev, 1964 True\nBeckman True\nYakovlev, 1977 True\nre-adaptation True\nhighest sport True\nTellier True\n-5 True\n+5 True\narithmetic product True\noutside the scope True\nsum of the motivational True","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"24260d24-17dd-44c4-8b61-a9303ebd0502","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:23.496Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"27: - Applied Performance's stated mission is to \"drive elite performance ... by leveraging science and technology through education and relationships,\" enabling the high-performance team to answer questions, ask better questions, and discover new ones — not to ha\n30: - Data collection i