{"parentUuid":"729a38cd-4fda-4d58-ac48-e1143ac11442","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"aa6bbad74ebe17310","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V3nqqMDHsFyyHiy39fSYDP","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**What it is** — Adaptation follows a load-dependent sequence with three outcomes: increasing, varied stimulus ⇒ adaptation ⇒ performance improvement; a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau ⇒ stagnation; an *excessive* load ⇒ maladaptation ⇒ decline (overreaching/overtraining). Improvement requires systematically crossing the current \"threshold of adaptation\" by raising intensity, volume, or frequency — then doing so again once the body has adapted.\n20:**When to apply** — The default logic for advancing any program; diagnosing a plateau (\"is the load still progressing, or has it gone standard?\"); justifying planned increases over a block.\n22:**When NOT to apply** — Not a license for linear, monotonic load increase indefinitely — that path leads to the maladaptation branch. The *rate* and *form* of progression must respect recovery and training age. Overload without adequate recovery is how you cross into overreaching. Pairs with [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] (recovery side) and is bounded by [[heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]].\n24:**Example** — A strength block uses loads >80% 1RM and nudges intensity/volume upward each microcycle; when testing shows a plateau, the coach changes the stimulus (exercise, tempo, rep scheme) rather than simply repeating the same successful load.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"15c66f90-cd0f-4ab4-bac2-9634138293ef","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:00:46.277Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"16:**What it is** — Adaptation follows a load-dependent sequence with three outcomes: increasing, varied stimulus ⇒ adaptation ⇒ performance improvement; a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau ⇒ stagnation; an *excessive* load ⇒ maladaptation ⇒ decline (overreaching/overtraining). Improvement requires systematically crossing the current \"threshold of adaptation\" by raising intensity, volume, or frequency — then doing so again once the body has adapted.\n20:**When to apply** — The default logic for advancing any program; diagnosing a plateau (\"is the load still progressing, or has it gone standard?\"); justifying planned increases over a block.\n22:**When NOT to apply** — Not a license for linear, monotonic load increase indefinitely — that path leads to the maladaptation branch. The *rate* and *form* of progression must respect recovery and training age. Overload without adequate recovery is how you cross into overreaching. Pairs with [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] (recovery side) and is bounded by [[heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]].\n24:**Example** — A strength block uses loads >80% 1RM and nudges intensity/volume upward each microcycle; when testing shows a plateau, the coach changes the stimulus (exercise, tempo, rep scheme) rather than simply repeating the same successful load.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"729a38cd-4fda-4d58-ac48-e1143ac11442","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
